]
`;

exports[`content > writes accessibility as markdown 1`] = `
"# Accessibility

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

zriz sets roles, keys and announcements. You write every word a screen reader says.

## Label every control

zriz ships no words. Give each control a name in your language:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
<Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten" />
<Cropper.Zoom aria-label="Zoom" />
<Cropper.AspectGroup aria-label="Aspect ratio">
  <Cropper.AspectItem value={1}>square</Cropper.AspectItem>
</Cropper.AspectGroup>
<Cropper.Rotate aria-label="Rotate left">
  rotate
</Cropper.Rotate>
\`\`\`

In development, \`Area\`, \`Dial\` and \`Zoom\` warn once each in the console when nothing names them. An \`aria-label\`, an \`aria-labelledby\` or a \`title\` counts. Text inside does not, because it never names a slider or a group. Nothing checks the aspect group or the buttons, so name those yourself. Buttons take their name from the text inside them.

## Check what a screen reader hears

| Part | What a screen reader gets |
| - | - |
| \`Area\` | Always \`role="application"\`, and one tab stop once a photo is placed, described by \`Instructions\`. The role passes the arrow keys through a screen reader's browse mode. Pass your own \`role\` to change it |
| \`Dial\` | A slider that reads "4 degrees" in your \`locale\`. \`format\` changes the wording |
| \`Zoom\` | A slider that reads "150%" in your \`locale\`. \`format\` changes the wording |
| \`AspectGroup\` | A \`radiogroup\` with one tab stop. Each \`AspectItem\` is a radio. Outside a group, an item is a button with \`aria-pressed\` |
| \`Rotate, Orientation, ZoomStep\` | Buttons. Name a \`ZoomStep\` with \`aria-label\`. \`Orientation\` is disabled for a free, original or square crop |
| \`Flip\` | A toggle button. \`aria-pressed\` follows \`mirrored\` |
| \`Reset\` | A button that is \`hidden\` while nothing has changed. If it has focus when it hides, focus moves to \`Area\`, or to the first control in \`Root\` that can take focus when there is no \`Area\` or it cannot take focus |
| \`Every control above\` | \`aria-disabled\` while \`Root\` is disabled. A press does nothing, and the control stays in the tab order |
| \`Image\` | An image named by its \`alt\`. Without \`alt\`, nothing |
| \`Handle, Grid, Mask, DialTicks, DialValue, ZoomThumb\` | Hidden |

## Describe the crop

\`Area\` points to \`Instructions\` with \`aria-describedby\`, so a screen reader reads its text when the crop gets focus:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Instructions>
  Arrows move the crop. Alt and arrows resize it.
</Cropper.Instructions>
\`\`\`

Render one \`Instructions\` and one \`Announcer\` per \`Root\`, outside any toolbar you duplicate. A second \`Instructions\` gets the same id, and \`Area\` only reads the first. A second \`Announcer\` calls \`announce\` again and speaks each message twice.

## Announce changes

A moving crop has no text, so a screen reader stays silent without \`Announcer\`. After each commit it calls \`announce\` and speaks what it returns, or nothing for \`null\`. This one speaks the position and width after each keyboard change. Dial and zoom keys commit as \`keyboard\` too, so it also speaks after an arrow press on either slider, on top of the slider's own value:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Announcer
  announce={({ center, size }, details) => {
    if (details.reason !== "keyboard") return null;
    const x = Math.round(center.x * 100);
    const y = Math.round(center.y * 100);
    const width = Math.round(size.width * 100);

    return \`Crop at \${x}%, \${y}%, \${width}% wide\`;
  }}
/>
\`\`\`

\`details.reason\` says what made the change, such as \`keyboard\`, \`dial\`, \`pan\` or \`orientation\`, and \`details.previous\` holds the crop before it. A message equal to the last one is spoken again.

## Move everything with keys

Every control works without a mouse. The keys live with each part: [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md), [dial](https://zriz.dev/docs/dial.md), [zoom](https://zriz.dev/docs/zoom.md) and [aspect ratio](https://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio.md). Buttons take Enter and Space like any button.

Escape also cancels a drag on the photo, a handle, \`Dial\` or \`Zoom\`, and the dialog around it stays open. A second Escape reaches the dialog.

A key pressed in a focusable child of \`Area\`, \`Dial\` or \`Zoom\` stays with that child, and Ctrl, Cmd and Alt shortcuts stay with the browser. The one exception is Alt + arrows on \`Area\`, which resizes the crop.

On keyboard focus, the stylesheet draws a two-tone ring inside \`Area\` in \`Canvas\` and \`CanvasText\`, so it shows on any photo and at padding 0. A mouse press adds nothing. Without the stylesheet, and on \`Dial\` and \`Zoom\`, the browser's default ring shows. [Crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md) shows how to recolour the ring.

## Work without a drag

The dial, the handles and the photo pan need a drag, and keys do not replace that for someone who only has a pointer. WCAG 2.5.7 asks for a way with single taps. \`Zoom\` already jumps on a press, for the rest add plain buttons that write to the cropper. Pass a reason, so \`Announcer\` speaks these writes too. The effect frees the photo, see [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md):

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { attachImage } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { useCropper } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useEffect } from "react";

export const Editor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const cropper = useCropper();

  useEffect(() => () => attachImage(cropper, null), [cropper]);

  const turn = (by: number) => {
    cropper.setRotation(cropper.getValue().rotation + by, {
      reason: "keyboard",
    });
  };

  const move = (x: number, y: number) => {
    cropper.panBy(x, y, { reason: "keyboard" });
  };

  const resize = (by: number) => {
    const state = cropper.getCropState();

    if (state) {
      const crop = {
        ...state.crop,
        height: state.crop.height * by,
        width: state.crop.width * by,
      };

      cropper.setCropState(
        { ...state, crop },
        { keep: "center", reason: "keyboard" }
      );
    }
  };

  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.Root cropper={cropper} src={src}>
        <Cropper.Viewport
          style={{ maxWidth: 480, height: 360 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button onClick={() => turn(-1)}>straighten left</button>
      <button onClick={() => turn(1)}>straighten right</button>
      <button onClick={() => move(-0.01, 0)}>move left</button>
      <button onClick={() => move(0.01, 0)}>move right</button>
      <button onClick={() => move(0, -0.01)}>move up</button>
      <button onClick={() => move(0, 0.01)}>move down</button>
      <button onClick={() => resize(0.9)}>smaller crop</button>
      <button onClick={() => resize(1.1)}>larger crop</button>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

## Respect reduced motion

With \`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce\`, motion that zriz starts on its own gets shorter. Drags, pinches and throws stay the same, so the photo follows your finger.

| Motion | Full | Reduced |
| - | - | - |
| Recentre | Spring, about 460 ms | Spring, about 250 ms |
| Rotate 90°, reset or undo across a turn | Spring, about 390 ms | 150 ms |
| Flip | Spring, about 420 ms | 150 ms |
| Aspect, orientation, reset or undo | Spring, about 460 ms | 150 ms |

\`motion="reduce"\` on \`Root\` forces the short motion, and \`motion="always"\` keeps the full one. \`Root\` shows \`data-reduced-motion\` while it is on.

Spring-backs keep their length in both modes: the dial past its limit, the photo pulled past the crop and the zoom past its limits.

The stylesheet's fades keep their length in both modes: the mask dims over 200 ms and comes back over 300 ms, and the grid fades in over 250 ms and out over 300 ms. After a gesture, the thirds grid and \`data-interacting\` on \`Root\` and \`Mask\` stay for 1380 ms. On \`Area\`, \`data-interacting\` clears when the gesture ends.

## API

### \`Announcer\`

Renders a visually hidden \`<div role="status" aria-live="polite">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. With a native modal \`<dialog>\`, keep it inside the dialog, because \`showModal\` makes everything outside inert and screen readers lose it.

- \`announce\` \`(value, details) => string | null\` required

  What to say after each commit. Return \`null\` to stay quiet

### \`Instructions\`

Renders a \`<div hidden>\` anywhere inside \`Root\`, which \`Area\` points to with \`aria-describedby\`. It has no props of its own. Its children are the text.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes aspect-ratio as markdown 1`] = `
"# Aspect ratio

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Buttons that lock the crop to a ratio such as 1:1 or 4:5, and one that swaps portrait and landscape.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, a track of free, original, 1:1, 4:5 and 16:9 with an orientation button, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Anatomy

The group and the orientation button go anywhere inside \`Root\`. Each item names one ratio:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.AspectGroup aria-label="Aspect ratio">
  <Cropper.AspectItem value="free">free</Cropper.AspectItem>
  <Cropper.AspectItem value={1}>1:1</Cropper.AspectItem>
  <Cropper.AspectItem value={4 / 5}>4:5</Cropper.AspectItem>
  <Cropper.AspectItem value={16 / 9}>16:9</Cropper.AspectItem>
</Cropper.AspectGroup>
<Cropper.Orientation aria-label="Swap orientation">
  <svg
    aria-hidden="true"
    width={20}
    height={20}
    viewBox="0 0 20 20"
  >
    <path
      d="M3 6h14v8H3z"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      strokeWidth={1.5}
    />
  </svg>
</Cropper.Orientation>
\`\`\`

\`value\` is width divided by height, so \`4 / 5\` is 4:5. \`"free"\` drops the lock and \`"original"\` uses the photo's own ratio. Picking an item springs the crop to it, with \`data-transition="morph"\` on \`Root\`.

On a round crop every item but \`value={1}\` is disabled. zriz ships these parts bare. The demo above adds \`"original"\`, turns its labels with the orientation as the ratios further down do, and uses the styles from the zriz site.

## Lock one ratio with no buttons

Pass \`aspect\` to \`Root\` and leave out the group. The crop keeps that ratio, and \`Reset\` returns to it:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={photo} aspect={16 / 9}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ height: 400 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## Use an item outside a group

Outside \`AspectGroup\`, an item is a plain button with \`aria-pressed\` instead of a radio:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.AspectItem value={1}>square</Cropper.AspectItem>
\`\`\`

## Turn the orientation icon

\`Orientation\` says which way the crop faces in \`data-orientation\`. This turns its icon for portrait:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="orientation"] {
  &[data-orientation="portrait"] svg {
    rotate: 90deg;
  }
}
\`\`\`

A press swaps the ratio for its inverse, so 4:5 becomes 5:4. It springs like an item pick, and \`onValueChange\` gets the reason \`orientation\`.

## Follow the orientation in the labels

After a swap the crop is 5:4, so a 4:5 item is no longer checked. To keep one item per ratio, flip the values and labels with the orientation. Render it inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperState } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";

const RATIOS = [
  [4, 5],
  [3, 4],
  [9, 16],
] as const;

export const Ratios = () => {
  const aspect = useCropperState((s) => s.aspect);
  const wide = typeof aspect === "number" && aspect > 1;

  return (
    <Cropper.AspectGroup aria-label="Aspect ratio">
      <Cropper.AspectItem value="free">free</Cropper.AspectItem>
      <Cropper.AspectItem value={1}>1:1</Cropper.AspectItem>
      {RATIOS.map(([w, h]) => (
        <Cropper.AspectItem
          key={\`\${w}:\${h}\`}
          value={wide ? h / w : w / h}
        >
          {wide ? \`\${h}:\${w}\` : \`\${w}:\${h}\`}
        </Cropper.AspectItem>
      ))}
    </Cropper.AspectGroup>
  );
};
\`\`\`

## Keyboard

The group is one tab stop. Tab lands on the checked item, or on the first one when none is checked. The arrows move and select at once:

| Key | What it does |
| - | - |
| \`right\` or \`down\` | Select the next ratio |
| \`left\` or \`up\` | Select the previous ratio |
| \`home\` or \`end\` | Select the first or the last ratio |

The arrows wrap around at the ends and skip disabled items. On a right-to-left page or with \`dir="rtl"\` on \`Root\`, left and right swap.

## API

### \`AspectGroup\`

Renders a \`<div role="radiogroup">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. Every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ aspect }\`.

- \`aria-label\` \`string\`

  Names the group for screen readers

### \`AspectItem\`

Renders a \`<button type="button">\`, with \`role="radio"\` inside \`AspectGroup\`. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ checked, disabled }\`.

- \`value\` \`"free" | "original" | number\` required

  The ratio this item picks, as width / height

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-checked\` | While this ratio is the current one |
| \`data-disabled\` | On a round crop for every value but 1, or when you pass \`disabled\` to the item |

### \`Orientation\`

Renders a \`<button type="button">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. It has no props of its own. It is \`disabled\` while the aspect is 1, \`"free"\` or \`"original"\`. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ orientation }\`.

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-orientation\` | \`"landscape"\` above 1, \`"portrait"\` below 1, \`"square"\` at 1 and while the aspect is \`"free"\` or \`"original"\` |
| \`data-disabled\` | While the aspect is 1, \`"free"\` or \`"original"\` |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes avatar as markdown 1`] = `
"# Avatar

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

A profile photo picker: the visitor picks a file, moves it under a circle and saves a round PNG.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/avatar runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo under a round crop, a zoom slider with zoom out and zoom in, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Take a file from the visitor

\`src\` takes the \`File\` from the input as it is. Picking a new file turns save off until that photo loads.

Each pick bumps a counter that \`Root\` takes as its \`key\`, so every photo starts from the full frame. [Loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) explains why. Safari fires no \`change\` for the same file twice, so \`onClick\` clears the input first.

\`\`\`tsx
<input
  aria-label="Choose a photo"
  type="file"
  accept="image/*"
  onClick={(event) => {
    event.currentTarget.value = "";
  }}
  onChange={(event) => {
    setStatus("empty");
    setSaveFailed(false);
    setPicks((count) => count + 1);
    setFile(event.target.files?.[0] ?? null);
  }}
/>
\`\`\`

## Crop to a circle

\`shape="circle"\` locks the aspect to 1 and draws a round mask. The area has no handles, so the visitor drags the photo under the circle and zooms it with a pinch or, with \`wheel="zoom"\`, the mouse wheel.

\`minSize\` keeps the crop at least 512 by 512 source pixels. On a photo with a side under 512 px, the circle stays as large as the photo allows. \`onLoad\` fires when the photo has decoded and turns save on.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  key={picks}
  actionsRef={actions}
  src={file}
  svg={false}
  shape="circle"
  minSize={{ width: 512, height: 512 }}
  onLoad={() => setStatus("ready")}
  onError={() => setStatus("failed")}
>
  <Cropper.Viewport
    wheel="zoom"
    style={{ width: 320, height: 320 }}
  >
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## Say when a photo does not open

\`onError\` gets a \`ZrizError\` when the file is not a photo or the browser cannot decode it, such as a HEIC file in Chrome. The alert asks for another file. [Errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists every code.

\`\`\`tsx
const unreadable =
  "this photo does not open. pick a jpeg, png or webp.";

{status === "failed" && <p role="alert">{unreadable}</p>}
\`\`\`

## Save a PNG

\`exportCrop\` reads the circle from \`Root\` and returns a PNG with transparent corners, at most 512 by 512. It comes from \`@zriz/core/dom\`.

An export can still fail on a very large photo, so \`save\` shows its own alert when it does.

\`running\` holds the export in flight. A second click does nothing, and the button keeps focus because \`saving\` sets \`aria-disabled\` and leaves it enabled. A new pick aborts the export, so an old crop never lands on the next photo. If an export finishes between two clicks, \`onSave\` runs twice, so keep it safe to repeat.

\`\`\`tsx
const unsaved = "this crop did not save. try again.";

const running = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);

useEffect(() => () => running.current?.abort(), [picks]);

const save = async () => {
  if (!actions.current || running.current) return;
  const controller = new AbortController();

  running.current = controller;
  setSaving(true);

  try {
    const { blob } = await exportCrop(actions.current, {
      maxSize: { width: 512, height: 512 },
      signal: controller.signal,
    });

    setSaveFailed(false);
    onSave(blob);
  } catch {
    setSaveFailed(!controller.signal.aborted);
  } finally {
    running.current = null;
    setSaving(false);
  }
};

{saveFailed && <p role="alert">{unsaved}</p>}

<button
  type="button"
  disabled={status !== "ready"}
  aria-disabled={saving}
  onClick={save}
>
  save
</button>
\`\`\`

## The whole file

\`AvatarPicker\` hands \`onSave\` the PNG as a \`Blob\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { exportCrop } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

type Props = { onSave: (png: Blob) => void };
type Status = "empty" | "ready" | "failed";

const unreadable =
  "this photo does not open. pick a jpeg, png or webp.";
const unsaved = "this crop did not save. try again.";

export const AvatarPicker = ({ onSave }: Props) => {
  const [file, setFile] = useState<File | null>(null);
  const [status, setStatus] = useState<Status>("empty");
  const [saveFailed, setSaveFailed] = useState(false);
  const [picks, setPicks] = useState(0);
  const [saving, setSaving] = useState(false);
  const actions = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const running = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => () => running.current?.abort(), [picks]);

  const save = async () => {
    if (!actions.current || running.current) return;
    const controller = new AbortController();

    running.current = controller;
    setSaving(true);

    try {
      const { blob } = await exportCrop(actions.current, {
        maxSize: { width: 512, height: 512 },
        signal: controller.signal,
      });

      setSaveFailed(false);
      onSave(blob);
    } catch {
      setSaveFailed(!controller.signal.aborted);
    } finally {
      running.current = null;
      setSaving(false);
    }
  };

  return (
    <>
      <input
        aria-label="Choose a photo"
        type="file"
        accept="image/*"
        onClick={(event) => {
          event.currentTarget.value = "";
        }}
        onChange={(event) => {
          setStatus("empty");
          setSaveFailed(false);
          setPicks((count) => count + 1);
          setFile(event.target.files?.[0] ?? null);
        }}
      />
      <Cropper.Root
        key={picks}
        actionsRef={actions}
        src={file}
        svg={false}
        shape="circle"
        minSize={{ width: 512, height: 512 }}
        onLoad={() => setStatus("ready")}
        onError={() => setStatus("failed")}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport
          wheel="zoom"
          style={{ width: 320, height: 320 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      {status === "failed" && <p role="alert">{unreadable}</p>}
      {saveFailed && <p role="alert">{unsaved}</p>}
      <button
        type="button"
        disabled={status !== "ready"}
        aria-disabled={saving}
        onClick={save}
      >
        save
      </button>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

The demo above runs this file with the styles from the zriz site. Its open, reset and download buttons take the place of the input, the alerts and save. It adds a zoom slider with step buttons, and \`maxZoom\` on \`Root\` stops the zoom at 3×.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes bundle-size as markdown 1`] = `
"# Bundle size

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

What each set of imports adds to your bundle, minified and compressed with Brotli.

## First load

| Set | Imports | Brotli |
| - | - | - |
| Editor | Every part from \`@zriz/react/cropper\` | 32.28 kB |
| Avatar | \`Root\`, \`Viewport\`, \`Image\`, \`Mask\`, \`Area\` | 25.67 kB |
| Root only | \`Root\` | 18.20 kB |
| Vanilla avatar | \`createCropper\`, \`loadImage\`, \`attachImage\`, \`bindViewport\`, \`bindPhoto\`, \`bindMask\` and \`bindArea\` | 21.10 kB |
| Server | \`toSharp\` from \`@zriz/core\` | 723 B |
| Stylesheet | \`styles.css\`, which is optional | 900 B |

Measured with size-limit 14.3.0 on the 0.0.0 packages. It bundles each set with Rolldown 1.2.15, minifies it with Oxc and leaves out React and react-dom. It compresses each output file with Brotli at quality 11, adds the files up and subtracts the 18 B that size-limit measures for an empty project. 1 kB is 1000 B. The build uses the \`production\` export condition, so development warnings do not count.

With gzip, the stylesheet is 1030 B.

The same sets built with Rollup 4, Vite 7 and 8, webpack 5 and Next 16, with Turbopack or webpack, come out between 2.2% smaller and 7.4% larger. esbuild comes out up to 16.3% larger.

esbuild ignores the \`production\` condition unless you pass it, so it ships the development warnings. Pass \`conditions: ["module", "production"]\` in production builds. \`module\` keeps the condition esbuild sets by default.

## Loaded later

Four chunks stay out of the first load and download the first time a photo needs them. Each row is what the chunk adds to the avatar set:

| Chunk | Loads when | Brotli |
| - | - | - |
| Render | The first export. It starts downloading once the first photo attaches | 2.30 kB |
| Sharpen | The sharper redraw at rest and on deep zoom. It starts downloading after the first paint | 546 B |
| SVG | The photo is an SVG | 891 B |
| Source | You pass an \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\`, \`OffscreenCanvas\` or \`ImageBitmap\` to \`loadImage\`, or your \`decode\` option runs after the browser fails to decode. \`Root\` never loads it | 557 B |

With all four chunks, even \`source\`, which \`Root\` never loads, the avatar set comes to 29.96 kB. Rolldown, Rollup, Vite, webpack and Next keep the code a chunk shares with the first load in the first load, so the chunks stay this small. esbuild splits that code into chunks of its own. A chunk bundled alone is larger: \`render\` is 3.68 kB.

## Dependencies

\`@zriz/react\` has \`@zriz/core\` and React as peers and no dependencies. \`@zriz/core\` has none either. Both packages mark only their CSS as a side effect, so Rollup, Vite, webpack and Next drop every part you do not import. esbuild drops their code too, but it still writes the four lazy chunks next to your bundle, even for a part such as \`Rotate\` that never loads them.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes buttons as markdown 1`] = `
"# Buttons

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`Rotate\` turns the photo a quarter turn, \`Flip\` mirrors it and \`Reset\` undoes every change.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/buttons runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, rotate left, rotate right, flip and flip vertical buttons, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Anatomy

Each one is a button that goes anywhere inside \`Root\`. Put an icon or a word inside, and give an icon-only button an \`aria-label\`:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Rotate aria-label="Rotate left">
  <svg
    aria-hidden="true"
    width={20}
    height={20}
    viewBox="0 0 20 20"
  >
    <path
      d="M4 4v4h4M4.5 8a6 6 0 1 1 1.5 6.5"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      strokeWidth={1.5}
    />
  </svg>
</Cropper.Rotate>
<Cropper.Flip aria-label="Flip">
  <svg
    aria-hidden="true"
    width={20}
    height={20}
    viewBox="0 0 20 20"
  >
    <path
      d="M10 3v14M7 5l-4 10h4zM13 5l4 10h-4z"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      strokeWidth={1.5}
    />
  </svg>
</Cropper.Flip>
<Cropper.Reset>reset</Cropper.Reset>
\`\`\`

zriz ships them bare, with no paint and no icons. The demo above adds a right turn and a vertical flip, and uses the icons and styles from the zriz site.

## See what each button moves

| Part | What it does |
| - | - |
| \`Rotate\` | Turns the photo 90° with a spring. A 3:2 crop becomes 2:3 |
| \`Flip\` | Mirrors the photo, left to right by default, with a spring |
| \`Reset\` | Springs back to where the crop started: \`defaultValue\` and the starting \`aspect\`, \`rotation\` and \`zoom\`, or the full photo |

Every button part sets \`touch-action: manipulation\` and \`user-select: none\` inline, so a double tap on a phone never zooms the page and a long press never selects the label and loses the click.

While \`Root\` is disabled, a press does nothing, and before the photo loads only \`Reset\` acts. The buttons stay natively enabled and report \`aria-disabled\`, so style them from \`data-disabled\` on \`Root\`, as [root](https://zriz.dev/docs/root.md) shows.

While a move runs, \`Root\` carries \`data-transition\`: \`"rotate"\`, \`"flip"\` or \`"morph"\` for \`Reset\`. A \`Reset\` across a quarter turn runs as \`"rotate"\`, and so does a \`Flip\` or \`Reset\` pressed while a rotate still runs. During \`"rotate"\` the crop turns whole with the photo, with no photo outside it, and the stylesheet hides \`Area\` and its handles. [Accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) lists how long each move takes, with and without reduced motion.

## Rotate both ways

\`Rotate\` turns left, like iOS Photos. \`direction="cw"\` turns right:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Rotate>rotate left</Cropper.Rotate>
<Cropper.Rotate direction="cw">rotate right</Cropper.Rotate>
\`\`\`

## Keep reset hidden

\`Reset\` hides with the \`hidden\` attribute while nothing has changed. If a class sets \`display\` on it, add this rule:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="reset"][hidden] {
  display: none;
}
\`\`\`

## API

### \`Rotate\`

Renders a \`<button type="button">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. Every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md).

- \`direction\` \`"ccw" | "cw"\` = \`"ccw"\`

  \`"ccw"\` turns left, \`"cw"\` turns right

### \`Flip\`

Renders a \`<button type="button">\` with \`aria-pressed\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ mirrored }\`.

- \`axis\` \`"x" | "y"\` = \`"x"\`

  \`"x"\` swaps left and right, \`"y"\` swaps top and bottom

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-mirrored\` | While the photo is mirrored, with \`aria-pressed="true"\` |

### \`Reset\`

Renders a \`<button type="button">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. It has no props of its own. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ pristine }\`.

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-pristine\` | While nothing has changed. The button is \`hidden\` then |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes changelog as markdown 1`] = `
"# Changelog

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

What changed in each published version of zriz, newest first.

## 0.0.0, not published yet

The first version. Nothing is on npm yet, so there is nothing to upgrade from. [Introduction](https://zriz.dev/docs/introduction.md) lists what it does.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes composition as markdown 1`] = `
"# Composition

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Render the zriz parts as your own components, and add your own handlers next to zriz's.

## Render your own element

Every part takes \`render\`. Pass an element, and zriz renders that element with the part's props, ref and data attributes merged in. Your component must spread the props it gets onto its root element and forward its ref. This one uses \`forwardRef\`, which works in React 18 and 19. In React 19 you can take \`ref\` as a plain prop instead. Save it as \`icon-button.tsx\`, which the examples below import:

\`\`\`tsx
import { forwardRef } from "react";
import type { ComponentPropsWithoutRef } from "react";

type IconButtonProps = ComponentPropsWithoutRef<"button"> & {
  icon: string;
};

export const IconButton = forwardRef<
  HTMLButtonElement,
  IconButtonProps
>(function IconButton({ icon, className, ...props }, ref) {
  return (
    <button
      ref={ref}
      className={\`icon-button \${className ?? ""}\`}
      data-icon={icon}
      {...props}
    />
  );
});
\`\`\`

Then pass it to a part:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Rotate
  aria-label="Rotate left"
  render={<IconButton icon="rotate-left" />}
/>
\`\`\`

The element's own props win over zriz's, except \`className\` and \`style\`, which merge, and event handlers, which both run. A prop set to \`undefined\` keeps zriz's value.

\`Handle\`, \`Grid\`, \`Mask\`, \`DialTicks\`, \`DialValue\` and \`ZoomThumb\` are \`aria-hidden\`, so render them as an element that can't take focus, such as a \`<span>\`. A \`<button>\` on \`Handle\` adds a tab stop that screen readers can't read.

## Change the element by state

Pass a function instead, and it gets the part's props and its state. Spread the props onto what you return, because they carry the ref and the handlers:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Flip
  aria-label="Flip"
  render={(props, state) => (
    <IconButton
      {...props}
      icon={state.mirrored ? "flip-on" : "flip"}
    />
  )}
/>
\`\`\`

Each part's state is in the API table on its page. \`className\` takes the same state, as shown in [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md).

## Stop zriz's own handler

Your handler runs first. Call \`preventZrizHandler(event)\` in it, and zriz skips that part's own handler for that event. \`Area\`, \`Image\`, \`Mask\` and \`Grid\` have no press handler of their own, so a press on them pans through \`Viewport\`. To stop that pan, veto on \`Viewport\` or call \`event.stopPropagation()\`. A press on \`Handle\`, \`Dial\`, \`Zoom\` or a button starts no pan:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Reset
  onClick={(event) => {
    if (!window.confirm("discard your changes?")) {
      preventZrizHandler(event);
    }
  }}
>
  reset
</Cropper.Reset>
\`\`\`

\`event.preventDefault()\` does not stop zriz. It only stops the browser default.

The wheel and Safari gestures on \`Viewport\` are native listeners. They run before your \`onWheel\`, so neither a veto nor \`event.stopPropagation()\` in it stops them. \`wheel="none"\` leaves a plain wheel to the page, and Ctrl + wheel still zooms.

\`preventZrizHandler\`, \`mergeProps\`, \`mergeRefs\` and \`CropperContext\` come from \`@zriz/react\`, like the hooks.

## Build a part of your own

A part of your own reads the crop with the hooks, or takes the cropper itself from \`CropperContext\`, and renders with \`useRender\`, which applies \`render\` and \`className\` the way the zriz parts do. [Hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md) shows one. \`mergeProps\` merges your props with a caller's and chains handlers the same way, and \`mergeRefs\` joins a ref you hold with the one a caller passes. \`CropperContext\` is \`null\` outside \`Root\`.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes crop-area as markdown 1`] = `
"# Crop area

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`Area\` is the crop you keep. \`Handle\` resizes it and \`Grid\` draws guides while you work.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo with eight handles and a grid on the crop, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Anatomy

\`Area\` goes in \`Viewport\`. \`Grid\` and the handles go inside it:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
  <Cropper.Grid />
  <Cropper.Handle position="top-left" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="top" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="top-right" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="right" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-right" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="bottom" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-left" />
  <Cropper.Handle position="left" />
</Cropper.Area>
\`\`\`

Leave out the handles you do not need. With \`shape="circle"\`, the stylesheet hides the four corner handles.

\`Area\` points \`aria-describedby\` at the \`Instructions\` part. Render it with your own help text. Without it, the reference points at nothing and screen readers skip it. [Accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) covers it and \`Announcer\`.

The demo above uses the styles from the zriz site.

## Recolour the focus ring

The stylesheet draws a two-tone ring inside \`Area\` on keyboard focus, so a crop flush with the viewport keeps it. Without the stylesheet, the browser draws its default ring. To recolour it:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="area"]:focus-visible {
  outline-color: #000;
}
[data-zriz-item="area"]:focus-visible::after {
  outline-color: #fdca00;
}
\`\`\`

## Make the handles easier to grab

Each handle's hit box is 44 px square, centred on the crop's edge. Set \`--zriz-handle-size\` on \`Area\` to change all eight. Give the viewport padding of at least half that size, or the clip cuts the box, see [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md):

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="viewport"] {
  padding: 28px;
}
[data-zriz-item="area"] {
  --zriz-handle-size: 56px;
}
\`\`\`

## Know when the grid shows

The grid hides at rest. It shows thirds (3 × 3) from the moment you press or move the crop, and a fine grid (9 × 9) while the dial moves:

| When | What shows |
| - | - |
| Press or move the crop | The thirds grid fades in, even if a press never moves. A drag, pinch, twist, wheel, handle, \`Zoom\` or a \`useZoom\` slider counts |
| Drag the dial or press a key on it | The fine grid fades in once the angle moves. A press that stays still shows none |
| Press a key on \`Area\` or \`Zoom\` | No new grid. A grid that already shows stays |
| Let go or stop moving the crop | It stays for a short hold, then fades out, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) |
| Let go of the dial | The fine grid fades out at once, or when a flick's glide stops |

## Make the straighten grid fainter

Lines of the fine grid carry no \`data-coarse\`. A fading grid keeps its lines, so this rule holds until the grid is gone:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="grid"] > :not([data-coarse]) {
  background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.3);
}
\`\`\`

## Keyboard

These keys work while \`Area\` has focus, never on the window:

| Key | What it does |
| - | - |
| \`arrow keys\` | Move the crop by 1% of its size |
| \`shift\` + \`arrow keys\` | Move it by 10% |
| \`alt\` + \`arrow keys\` | Resize the crop by 1%, or 10% with Shift. Right and down grow it, left and up shrink it. A locked aspect keeps its ratio. A free crop changes that side only, and stops at the photo edge, \`minSize\`, \`maxZoom\` or 88 px on screen. The centre stays put |
| \`+\` or \`=\` | Zoom in 1.1× |
| \`-\` or \`_\` | Zoom out 1.1× |
| \`0\` | Jump back to where \`Reset\` goes, with no spring |

Escape during a handle drag cancels it and puts the crop back.

## API

### \`Area\`

Renders a \`<div role="application">\` inside \`Viewport\`, with \`tabIndex={0}\` once a photo is placed and \`tabIndex={-1}\` until then, so tab skips an empty crop. zriz writes its position and size inline on every frame. Every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ shape, interacting }\`.

- \`aria-label\` \`string\`

  Names the crop for screen readers, such as \`"Crop area"\`. Development builds warn without it
- \`liveVariables\` \`boolean\` = \`false\`

  Writes the variables below on \`Area\` every frame

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-shape\` | \`"rect"\` or \`"circle"\`, from \`Root\` |
| \`data-visible\` | While a photo is placed |
| \`data-interacting\` | During a gesture, and while a zriz key is held down |

| Variable | What it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-line\` | Paints the frame and the handle marks. [Styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its light and dark values |
| \`--zriz-rotation\` | With \`liveVariables\`, the straighten angle in degrees, like −4.5 |
| \`--zriz-rotation-progress\` | With \`liveVariables\`, the angle over its limit, from −1 to 1 |
| \`--zriz-zoom\` | With \`liveVariables\`, the zoom, 1 to \`maxZoom\` |
| \`--zriz-zoom-progress\` | With \`liveVariables\`, the zoom on a log scale, 0 to 1 |
| \`--zriz-overscroll\` | With \`liveVariables\`, how far in px a drag pulls the photo past an edge. 0 at rest |

### \`Handle\`

Renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` inside \`Area\`, so a \`render\` element must not take focus. A drag stops at the photo edge, the viewport's padding and \`maxZoom\`. Each side also stops at 88 px on screen or \`minSize\`, whichever is larger. A side already smaller stays put and can only grow. The stylesheet hides the corner handles on a circle only when they sit right inside \`Area\`. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ position, active }\`.

- \`position\` \`HandleProps["position"]\` required

  The corner or edge it drags: \`"top-left"\`, \`"top"\`, \`"top-right"\`, \`"right"\`, \`"bottom-right"\`, \`"bottom"\`, \`"bottom-left"\`, \`"left"\`

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-edge\` | Always, set to its position |
| \`data-active\` | From a press on this handle until the pointer lets go, even if it never moves |

| Variable | What it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-handle-size\` | The hit box, 44 px square unless you set it |

### \`Grid\`

Renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` of \`<span>\` lines inside \`Area\`. It has no props of its own. The stylesheet gives each line 1 px. Without it, set \`min-width\` and \`min-height\` yourself. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ grid }\`.

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-grid\` | \`"coarse"\` for thirds, \`"fine"\` or \`"none"\` |
| \`data-visible\` | While a grid shows |
| \`data-axis\` | \`"x"\` on vertical lines, \`"y"\` on horizontal ones |
| \`data-coarse\` | On each line of the thirds grid |

| Variable | What it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-grid\` | Paints the grid lines. [Styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its default |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes cropper as markdown 1`] = `
"# Cropper

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`createCropper\` makes the store behind every \`Root\`. Use it for [vanilla JS](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md), or to test your crop logic without a browser.

Import it from \`@zriz/core\`, installed as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md), give it the photo's size and write to it:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper({
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
});

cropper.setRotation(7);
\`\`\`

A write changes \`getValue()\` at once. \`change\` and \`commit\` fire on the next frame. In React, \`Root\` makes the cropper for you, and [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md) shows how to reach it.

## Options

- \`image\` \`Size | null\` = \`null\`

  Sets the photo's natural size. Most writes wait for it. A side of about 1e18 or more can drop the tilt and open level
- \`value\` \`CropValue\`

  Sets the start in place of \`defaultValue\`. \`reset()\` still goes to \`defaultValue\`
- \`defaultValue\` \`Partial<CropValue>\`

  Merges over the core \`defaultValue\`. The cropper starts here and \`reset()\` returns here. Read once
- \`minSize\` \`Size\` = \`{ width: 1, height: 1 }\`

  Sets the smallest crop in source pixels, along the photo's own sides
- \`maxZoom\` \`number\` = \`15\`

  Sets the largest zoom. 1 is the largest crop
- \`focalLength\` \`number\` = \`26\`

  Sets the lens as a 35 mm equivalent, for perspective correction. At a strong tilt, zriz lengthens a lens too short to keep the photo in front of it. A length that overflows falls back to 26 mm
- \`disabled\` \`boolean\` = \`false\`

  Refuses gestures and most writes
- \`clock\` \`Clock\` = \`frameClock()\`

  Schedules frames. Tests pass a manual one, see test without a browser below
- \`onValueChange\` \`(value, details) => void\`

  Runs at most once per frame while the crop moves
- \`onValueCommit\` \`(value, details) => void\`

  Runs when a gesture or write commits
- \`plugins\` \`readonly CropperPlugin[]\` = \`[]\`

  Adds behaviour, see add a plugin and clean it up below

\`minSize\` also caps the angle once its diagonal is longer than a side of the photo. Only its size next to the photo counts, not the photo's pixel count. While \`disabled\`, \`setValue()\`, \`setImage()\` and \`setOptions()\` still work. Both callbacks are on [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md).

## Read the crop

- \`getValue\` \`() => CropValue\`

  Returns the live [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md), frames inside a gesture included
- \`getState\` \`() => CropperSnapshot\`

  Returns the snapshot that \`useCropperState\` reads, see [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md)
- \`getCropState\` \`() => CropState | null\`

  Returns the crop in image pixels, or \`null\` before a photo
- \`getZoom\` \`() => number\`

  Returns the zoom, 1 before a photo loads. 1 is the largest crop that fits
- \`getMaxZoom\` \`() => number\`

  Returns the \`maxZoom\` option, 15 by default
- \`getRotationLimit\` \`() => number\`

  Returns 45, or less when \`minSize\` leaves too little room
- \`getPixelCrop\` \`(scale?) => ExportPlan | null\`

  Returns the export plan, or \`null\` before a photo, see [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md)

## Change the crop

- \`setValue\` \`(value, options?) => void\`

  Merges the fields you pass into the current value
- \`setRotation\` \`(degrees, options?) => void\`

  Straightens the photo
- \`setZoom\` \`(zoom, options?) => void\`

  Zooms around \`anchor\`, a point from 0 to 1 on the photo, or the crop's centre
- \`panBy\` \`(dx, dy, options?) => void\`

  Moves the crop by fractions of the photo's width and height
- \`setAspect\` \`(aspect, options?) => void\`

  Locks the crop to an aspect
- \`setCropState\` \`(state, options?) => void\`

  Sets the crop in image pixels. \`keep: "center"\` holds the centre. The default, \`keep: "size"\`, holds the size. A state from a photo of another size does nothing, and the state's \`aspect\` and \`focal\` are ignored
- \`rotate90\` \`(direction?, options?) => void\`

  Turns counter-clockwise by default, or clockwise with \`"cw"\`. A numeric aspect turns with it, so 4:3 becomes 3:4
- \`flip\` \`(axis?, options?) => void\`

  Mirrors left to right by default. \`"y"\` mirrors top to bottom
- \`reset\` \`(options?) => void\`

  Returns to \`defaultValue\`, with or without a photo
- \`setImage\` \`(size | null) => void\`

  Sets the photo's natural size. A new size refits the crop with reason \`coverage\`. A size that is not finite and positive counts as \`null\`
- \`setOptions\` \`(options) => void\`

  Changes \`disabled\`, \`maxZoom\`, \`minSize\`, \`focalLength\` and the two callbacks. The type takes only those six. \`image\`, \`value\`, \`defaultValue\`, \`clock\` and \`plugins\` stay as created. A new \`focalLength\` takes effect on the next \`setValue()\`, \`reset()\` or \`setImage()\`. Call \`setImage()\` for a new photo

Every write keeps the crop valid, see [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md). Before the cropper has a photo, only \`setValue()\`, \`reset()\`, \`setImage()\` and \`setOptions()\` do anything.

## Tell listeners why

Every write except \`setImage()\` and \`setOptions()\` takes action options last. Outside a gesture, \`setValue()\`, \`setRotation()\`, \`setZoom()\` and \`setCropState()\` report \`imperative\` unless you pass a reason. Here the write straightens the photo 5°, and on the next frame the commit logs \`5 history\`:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper({
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
  onValueCommit: (value, details) => {
    console.log(value.rotation, details.reason);
  },
});

cropper.setRotation(5, { reason: "history" });
\`\`\`

- \`reason\` \`ChangeReason\`

  Names the change for listeners, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md)
- \`event\` \`Event\`

  Passes the DOM event behind the change
- \`emit\` \`boolean\`

  With \`false\`, no change or commit fires, and the new value counts as committed. It also drops a change still waiting from the same frame

## Drive the crop from your own input

Call \`begin()\` when your input takes hold and \`end()\` when it lets go. Every write between them is one gesture with one commit. Pass the same \`owner\` object to both, so no other input can end your gesture:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper({
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
});
const owner = {};

export const bind = (input: HTMLInputElement) => {
  input.addEventListener("pointerdown", (event) => {
    cropper.begin("dial", event, owner);
  });

  input.addEventListener("input", () => {
    cropper.setRotation(input.valueAsNumber);
  });

  const end = (event: PointerEvent) => {
    cropper.end(event, owner);
  };

  input.addEventListener("pointerup", end);
  input.addEventListener("pointercancel", end);
};
\`\`\`

- \`begin\` \`(reason, event?, owner?) => boolean\`

  Starts a gesture and stops any animation that holds the commit. Returns \`false\` when the cropper is disabled or another owner holds a gesture. A dial glide or a wheel turn gives way to it
- \`end\` \`(event?, owner?) => void\`

  Ends the gesture. The commit follows once blocking tasks stop
- \`cancel\` \`(event?, owner?) => void\`

  Ends the gesture and restores the crop from before it
- \`run\` \`(task, blocking?) => () => void\`

  Calls \`task(time, delta)\` every frame until it returns \`false\`. Blocking tasks, the default, hold the commit back. Returns a cancel function
- \`invalidate\` \`() => void\`

  Asks for one more \`frame\` event
- \`on\` \`(type, listener) => () => void\`

  Listens to one event, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md). Returns a remove function
- \`subscribe\` \`(listener) => () => void\`

  Runs the listener when the snapshot changes

## Test without a browser

In Node, the default clock waits about 16 ms per frame on \`setTimeout\`. Pass a clock of your own and step the frames yourself, so every event fires when your test says:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";
import type { Clock } from "@zriz/core";

const queue = new Map<number, (time: number) => void>();
let id = 0;
let now = 0;

const clock: Clock = {
  request: (step) => {
    id += 1;
    queue.set(id, step);

    return id;
  },
  cancel: (handle) => {
    queue.delete(handle);
  },
};

const advance = (ms: number) => {
  now += ms;
  const steps = [...queue.values()];

  queue.clear();
  for (const step of steps) step(now);
};

const commits: number[] = [];
const cropper = createCropper({
  clock,
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
  onValueCommit: (value) => commits.push(value.rotation),
});

cropper.setRotation(7);
advance(16);
console.log(commits);
\`\`\`

\`setRotation()\` changes \`getValue()\` at once, and \`advance(16)\` runs the frame that sends the commit. The log reads \`[7]\`.

## Add a plugin and clean it up

A plugin is an object with a \`name\`, an \`init\` and an optional \`destroy\`. \`init\` runs right after \`createCropper\` makes the cropper. \`Root\` and \`useCropper\` take \`plugins\` too, read them once and run \`init\` once on the client, after mount, never during a render.

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";
import type { CropperPlugin } from "@zriz/core";

let off = () => {};

const logger: CropperPlugin = {
  name: "logger",
  init: (cropper) => {
    off = cropper.on("commit", (value) => console.log(value));
  },
  destroy: () => off(),
};

const cropper = createCropper({ plugins: [logger] });

cropper.destroy();
\`\`\`

\`destroy\` runs only when you call \`destroy()\`. \`Root\` never calls it. When the component that made the cropper unmounts, \`Root\` or the one that called \`useCropper\`, it calls \`stop()\` instead, on purpose: \`StrictMode\` and \`<Activity>\` unmount a component and mount the same cropper again, and a destroyed cropper cannot come back.

If a plugin holds a timer or a socket, call \`destroy()\` yourself when you are done with the cropper, for example through \`actionsRef\`.

- \`stop\` \`() => void\`

  Ends any gesture, drops all motion, commits what is there and emits \`stop\`. The cropper keeps working
- \`destroy\` \`() => void\`

  Runs each plugin's \`destroy\`, emits \`destroy\`, detaches the photo and drops every listener. A change or commit still waiting is dropped, and a running gesture ends with no \`gestureend\`, so call \`stop()\` first to send them. The cropper is finished, and every later write is ignored

## Read the crop in image pixels

\`getCropState()\` and \`useCropperFrame\` give a \`CropState\`, and \`setCropState()\` takes one. \`crop.x\` and \`crop.y\` are not a corner. They are the crop's centre measured from the photo's centre, so they can be negative, with x to the right and y down, after the quarter turns.

- \`image\` \`Size\`

  The photo's size before any turn
- \`turns\` \`0 | 1 | 2 | 3\`

  Quarter turns, 0 to 3
- \`flip\` \`boolean\`

  \`true\` while mirrored
- \`angle\` \`number\`

  The straighten angle in degrees
- \`crop\` \`{ x, y, width, height }\`

  The crop in image pixels, after the quarter turns
- \`aspect\` \`number | null\`

  The ratio as a number, or \`null\` when free
- \`perspective\` \`{ vertical, horizontal, focal }\`

  The tilt in degrees, with \`focal\` in pixels
"
`;

exports[`content > writes dial as markdown 1`] = `
"# Dial

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

The ruler that levels a tilted photo, up to 45° each way, like the one in iOS Photos.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/dial runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, the straighten dial with its value, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Anatomy

Put \`DialTicks\` inside \`Dial\`. \`Dial\` and \`DialValue\` go anywhere inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.DialValue />
<Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten">
  <Cropper.DialTicks />
</Cropper.Dial>
\`\`\`

The stylesheet makes \`Dial\` 40 px tall and clips its ticks at the sides only, so the zero dot above them stays in view. It draws the ticks and the centre line. \`DialValue\` ships bare.

The demo above shows the angle to one decimal with a degree sign and uses the styles from the zriz site.

## Drag and flick the ruler

The first 10 px of a drag only pick up the ruler. After that, every 4 px turns 1°. A quick flick keeps it gliding after you let go, and past the limit it stretches and springs back. On touch, a vertical swipe scrolls the page instead.

The photo zooms in as the angle grows. One gesture back to 0° restores the zoom. Separate gestures keep it, as in iOS Photos, until \`Reset\` or \`Zoom\` takes it back.

## Set the ruler width

\`Dial\` fills its parent's width. Ticks sit 9 px apart, one every 2.25°, so 361 px fits all 41 ticks:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten" style={{ maxWidth: 361 }}>
  <Cropper.DialTicks />
</Cropper.Dial>
\`\`\`

The demo above is 360 px wide, as on the zriz site, so its outermost ticks fall on the edges.

## Add a degree sign

\`format\` on \`DialValue\` gets the angle in degrees and returns the text to show:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.DialValue
  format={(angle) =>
    \`\${Math.sign(angle) * Math.round(Math.abs(angle))}°\`
  }
/>
\`\`\`

## Make the major ticks taller

Ticks carry no \`data-zriz-item\`. Select them through \`DialTicks\`:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="dial-ticks"] > [data-major] {
  height: 14px;
}
\`\`\`

## Change the drag colour

The centre line takes the accent colour while you drag. \`--zriz-accent\` sets it, and [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its default:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="dial"] {
  --zriz-accent: #0a84ff;
}
\`\`\`

## Keyboard

These keys work while \`Dial\` has focus:

| Key | What it does |
| - | - |
| \`left\` or \`right\` | Lower or raise the angle by 1° |
| \`up\` or \`down\` | Raise or lower the angle by 1° |
| \`shift\` + \`arrow keys\` | The same by 10° |
| \`page up\` or \`page down\` | Raise or lower the angle by 10° |
| \`home\` or \`end\` | Go to the lowest or the highest angle, −45° or 45° at most |
| \`0\` | Straighten to 0° |

On a right-to-left page or with \`dir="rtl"\` on \`Root\`, left and right swap. Up and down stay the same. The ticks read the direction when they draw, so after a page flips its \`dir\` they follow at the next draw. A new \`dir\` on \`Root\` redraws them at once.

Escape during a drag cancels it and puts the angle back.

## API

### \`Dial\`

Renders a \`<div role="slider">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. Every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ dragging }\`.

- \`aria-label\` \`string\`

  Names the slider for screen readers. Development builds warn without it
- \`format\` \`Intl.NumberFormatOptions\`

  How screen readers hear the angle, merged over the default \`"5 degrees"\`. An \`aria-valuetext\` of yours wins over it

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-dragging\` | While a pointer drags any \`Dial\` of this cropper, so a second copy lights up too |
| \`data-snapped\` | While the angle rests at exactly 0°, from the start too. The zero dot hides then. A drag through 0° leaves it as it was until you let go |

| Variable | What it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-line\` | Paints the centre line, the zero dot and the major ticks. [Styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its light and dark values |
| \`--zriz-accent\` | Paints the centre line while you drag. [Styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its default |

### \`DialTicks\`

Renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` of \`<span>\` ticks inside \`Dial\`. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ count }\`.

- \`count\` \`number\` = \`41\`

  How many ticks to draw, 2.25° apart. 41 spans 45° each way. Use an odd count so one tick sits at 0°

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-major\` | Every tenth tick from the centre: 0°, 22.5° and 45° each way |
| \`data-zero\` | The centre tick, at 0° |
| \`data-fade\` | \`"0"\`, \`"1"\` or \`"2"\` on the last three ticks at each end, outermost \`"0"\` |

| Variable | What it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-tick\` | Paints the minor ticks. [Styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its light and dark values |
| \`--zriz-tick-index\` | On each tick, its index from the left, from 0 to \`count\` minus 1 |

### \`DialValue\`

Renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<span>\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. zriz writes its text, so it takes no children, and neither does a \`render\` element.

- \`format\` \`(angle: number) => string\`

  Turns the angle into the text shown. The default rounds it and adds no unit
"
`;

exports[`content > writes errors as markdown 1`] = `
"# Errors

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Every error zriz throws or reports, when it happens and how to fix it.

## Where errors arrive

\`Root\` calls \`onError\` with a \`ZrizError\` when a photo cannot load. \`ZrizError\` comes from \`@zriz/core/dom\`, like \`loadImage\` and \`exportCrop\`. \`attachImage\` throws \`no-image\` at once when the photo was already freed. \`loadImage\` and \`exportCrop\` reject, so catch them where you call them:

\`\`\`ts
import type { Cropper } from "@zriz/core";
import { exportCrop, ZrizError } from "@zriz/core/dom";

export const save = async (
  cropper: Cropper,
  signal?: AbortSignal
) => {
  try {
    const { blob } = await exportCrop(cropper, { signal });

    return blob;
  } catch (error) {
    if (
      error instanceof ZrizError &&
      error.code === "aborted"
    ) {
      return null;
    }

    throw error;
  }
};
\`\`\`

Every \`ZrizError\` has a \`code\` from the table below, and its message starts with \`zriz:\`. The one exception is a \`decode\` function you pass to \`loadImage\`: when it throws, \`loadImage\` rejects with your own error as it is.

## Codes

| Code | When it fires, and the fix |
| - | - |
| \`cross-origin\` | The server sent the photo without \`Access-Control-Allow-Origin\`, the network dropped, \`connect-src\` in a content security policy blocked the URL, or an \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\` or \`ImageBitmap\` you pass is tainted. Send the header. If the request needs cookies, pass \`crossOrigin="use-credentials"\` to \`Root\`. Add the host to \`connect-src\`, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) |
| \`unsupported-format\` | The browser could not decode a HEIC, JPEG XL or TIFF file, because it does not support the format or the file is damaged, either on load or when an export decodes the original again. Try another copy of the file. Call \`loadImage\` yourself with your own \`decode\` function, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md), or convert the file to JPEG first |
| \`decode\` | The URL answered with an error status, the file cannot be read, the file is cut short, the bytes are not an image, Safari refused a BMP with a v4 or v5 header, a zriz chunk failed to download, an \`img\`, \`video\` or \`canvas\` you pass cannot be read, a \`video\` has no current frame, \`img-src\` in a content security policy blocked an SVG or a display-p3 export, the browser gave no 2D canvas, or an export could not decode the original again. Open the URL and check the response. If you are offline, try again once the network is back. If the same error comes back, reload the page, because a browser can keep a failed chunk until then. Wait for \`loadeddata\` before you pass a \`video\`. [Loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) lists the \`img-src\` sources |
| \`too-large\` | A file's header or decoded image has more pixels than \`maxPixels\`, 268402689 by default. This also covers a \`decode\` function, but not an SVG or an \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\`, \`OffscreenCanvas\` or \`ImageBitmap\` source. Send a smaller file. To open it anyway, load the photo yourself with a higher \`maxPixels\`, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) |
| \`timeout\` | A URL sent no bytes for 15 s, a photo took over 15 s to decode after its last byte, or an export took over 30 s. Time spent waiting behind other photos or exports does not count. Send a smaller file or export a smaller \`maxSize\`. For a slow network, load the photo yourself with a longer \`timeout\`, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) |
| \`aborted\` | The \`signal\` you passed fired, a new photo or a detach came while the export code was still downloading, or a photo from an \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\`, SVG or \`decode\` function was freed while the export waited its turn. A new photo after that does not stop the export and you get the old crop, so abort with your own \`signal\` when the photo changes. \`Root\` also aborts its own load when \`src\` changes and never reports it. Nothing. Skip this code in your catch |
| \`blank\` | The browser drew an empty canvas at the export size, then at half and a quarter of it. This is the canvas memory limit, mostly on phones. Set \`maxSize\` to 2048 by 2048 or less |
| \`no-image\` | An export ran before a photo loaded, the app attached a photo that was already freed, or the app has two copies of \`@zriz/core\` and the export came from the one that never got the photo. A detach before the export call, even with an aborted signal, gives this code. A detach in the same tick right after the call gives \`aborted\`. Disable the export button until \`onLoad\` fires. Load a freed photo again. Keep \`@zriz/core\` at the same version as \`@zriz/react\` |

## Thrown errors

These are plain \`Error\` objects that React shows in its error overlay. The first three mean a part or hook cannot find its cropper. \`toSharp\` throws the last one as a \`RangeError\`, usually on your server.

- \`zriz: parts must be inside <Cropper.Root>\`

  Move the part inside \`Root\`. A part in a portal still needs \`Root\` above it in the React tree
- \`zriz: pass a cropper or render inside <Cropper.Root>\`

  A hook such as \`useCropperValue\` or \`useZoom\` ran outside \`Root\`. Pass it the cropper from \`useCropper\`, or move the component inside \`Root\`
- \`zriz: no cropper\`

  \`Root\` got \`cropper\` on its first render and none later. Keep passing the same cropper, or give \`Root\` a new \`key\` to start a new one
- \`zriz: this is not an export plan\`

  Pass \`toSharp\` the plan from \`getPixelCrop()\` or a \`commit\`, and the size of the same photo, both unchanged. When \`pixelRect\` is \`null\`, a bad \`forward\`, a side that is not a whole number of at least 1, or a crop fully outside the photo throws this. A plan with a \`pixelRect\` gets no check, so recompute the plan on your server as [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) does

## Console warnings

In development, \`Area\`, \`Dial\` and \`Zoom\` each warn once when nothing names them, \`Dial\` and \`Zoom\` warn once for a \`locale\` or \`format\` the browser refuses, and a second visible \`Viewport\` on one cropper warns once. The message has the part's name. Production builds print nothing.

- \`zriz: <Cropper.Area> needs aria-label or aria-labelledby. zriz ships no words, so screen readers get nothing otherwise.\`

  Name the part. [Accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) shows how to label each part
- \`zriz: <Cropper.Dial> ignores a locale or format the browser refuses. A refused locale falls back to the browser's, and a refused format to the default value text.\`

  Pass a locale tag like \`en-US\` and options \`Intl.NumberFormat\` accepts. Until then a refused locale speaks in the browser's locale and a refused format speaks the default text
- \`zriz: one <Cropper.Viewport> per cropper. A second one takes over the layout. Draw a live preview with useCropperFrame.\`

  Render one \`Viewport\` per cropper, and draw a live preview with \`useCropperFrame\`, see [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md)
"
`;

exports[`content > writes events as markdown 1`] = `
"# Events

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Two callbacks on \`Root\` report crop changes: \`onValueChange\` for live UI and \`onValueCommit\` for saving.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  onValueChange={(value) => setAngle(value.rotation)}
  onValueCommit={(value) => save(value)}
/>
\`\`\`

\`onValueChange\` runs at most once per frame while the crop moves. \`onValueCommit\` runs once when a gesture or a write is done. \`createCropper\` takes the same two options.

## Save when the user lets go

A drag, a pinch or a turn of the dial commits once, when the finger lifts. A thrown dial commits when it stops. A key on \`Area\`, \`Dial\` or \`Zoom\` commits once when it comes up, however long it was held, or when focus leaves. A write such as \`rotate90()\` commits on the next frame, and the turn you see animates after it. A gesture that ends where it started does not commit. The first photo, or a photo after \`null\`, fits the crop without a commit. A later photo commits only when fitting it changes the value, such as a locked aspect on a photo of another shape, and that commit has the reason \`coverage\`. To save a photo nobody touched, read \`getValue()\` or \`getPixelCrop()\` from the cropper in \`actionsRef\`, see [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md).

\`stop()\` and unmount commit synchronously. React warns if \`onValueCommit\` calls \`flushSync\` inside that unmount, so leave \`flushSync\` out of the handler.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  onValueCommit={(value, details) => {
    upload(value, details.pixelCrop);
  }}
/>
\`\`\`

## Follow one gesture from start to commit

Log both callbacks, then throw the dial:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  onValueChange={(_, d) => console.log(d.reason, d.phase)}
  onValueCommit={(_, d) => console.log(d.reason, "commit")}
/>
\`\`\`

| Log | When |
| - | - |
| \`dial start\` | The first frame the dial turns |
| \`dial move\` | Every later frame of the drag and of the throw |
| \`dial end\` | The last frame of the throw |
| \`dial commit\` | \`onValueCommit\`, in the same frame |

A drag that stops before the finger lifts has no \`end\`, and its last \`move\` goes straight to the commit. A write outside a gesture logs one \`instant\` change, then its commit.

## Reject a change

Call \`details.cancel()\` and the crop snaps back to where the last change left it. This keeps the angle within 10° each way:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  onValueChange={(value, details) => {
    if (Math.abs(value.rotation) > 10) details.cancel();
  }}
/>
\`\`\`

## Change details

The second argument of \`onValueChange\`:

- \`reason\` \`ChangeReason\`

  Names what moved the crop, see reasons below
- \`phase\` \`ChangePhase\`

  Is \`start\`, \`move\`, \`end\` or \`instant\`, see the order above
- \`changed\` \`readonly ValueField[]\`

  Lists the fields that moved. Never empty
- \`previous\` \`CropValue\`

  Holds what the last change left
- \`event\` \`Event | undefined\`

  Holds the DOM event behind the change
- \`cancel\` \`() => void\`

  Rejects this change
- \`isCanceled\` \`boolean\`

  Is \`true\` once \`cancel()\` ran

## Commit details

The second argument of \`onValueCommit\`:

- \`reason\` \`ChangeReason\`

  Names the gesture or write behind the commit
- \`previous\` \`CropValue\`

  Holds the last committed value. Push it to a stack for [undo](https://zriz.dev/docs/undo.md)
- \`pixelCrop\` \`ExportPlan | null\`

  Holds the export plan at scale 1 in source pixels, or \`null\` before a photo loads. Use it for your own tools. [Server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) sends the value from \`getValue()\` and recomputes the plan on the server, see [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md)
- \`event\` \`Event | undefined\`

  Holds the event that ended the gesture, or the one passed with the write

## Reasons

Every change and commit names its reason. Inside a gesture, a write without its own reason reports the gesture's reason.

| Reason | Sent by |
| - | - |
| \`pan\` | One finger or pointer dragging the photo, and \`panBy()\` |
| \`pinch\` | Two fingers pinching or twisting, or a trackpad pinch in Safari |
| \`wheel\` | A mouse wheel or trackpad scrolling or zooming the photo |
| \`resize\` | A handle being dragged |
| \`dial\` | The dial being dragged or thrown |
| \`keyboard\` | Keys on \`Area\` and \`Dial\`. Keys on \`Zoom\` report \`zoom-control\` on each change and \`keyboard\` on the commit |
| \`zoom-control\` | The \`Zoom\` slider, \`ZoomStep\` buttons and \`useZoom\` |
| \`aspect\` | \`AspectItem\` buttons, \`setAspect()\` and the \`aspect\` prop |
| \`orientation\` | The \`Orientation\` button, which swaps portrait and landscape |
| \`rotate-90\` | \`Rotate\` buttons and \`rotate90()\` |
| \`flip\` | \`Flip\` buttons and \`flip()\` |
| \`reset\` | \`Reset\` and \`reset()\` |
| \`coverage\` | A later photo of another size, or new options, that refit the crop. The first photo sends nothing |
| \`history\` | Your own writes only. zriz never sends it, so [undo](https://zriz.dev/docs/undo.md) can skip those writes. \`Root\` animates them like \`reset()\` |
| \`controlled\` | The \`rotation\` and \`zoom\` props, on the commit and on a change when zriz applied another value. A new \`value\` prop sends nothing |
| \`imperative\` | \`setValue()\`, \`setRotation()\`, \`setZoom()\` and \`setCropState()\` called outside a gesture without a reason |

## Listen on the cropper

The cropper emits the two callbacks as \`change\` and \`commit\`, plus six more events. Under \`Root\` or \`useCropper\`, \`change\` also fires when a controlled prop lands as passed, which \`onValueChange\` skips. In React, make the cropper with \`useCropper\` and listen in an effect: \`useEffect(() => cropper.on("settle", onSettle), [cropper])\`, see [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md). \`createCropper\` comes from \`@zriz/core\`, installed as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md). \`on()\` returns a function that removes the listener:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper();
const off = cropper.on("settle", () => {
  console.log("the crop is still");
});

off();
\`\`\`

| Event | Listener gets | What it does |
| - | - | - |
| \`gesturestart\` | \`reason, event\` | Fires when an input takes hold |
| \`gestureend\` | \`reason, event\` | Fires when \`end()\` runs. For a thrown dial, that is when the throw stops |
| \`settle\` | \`nothing\` | Fires each time the last animation stops, springs included. \`stop()\` ends motion with no \`settle\`, so listen to \`stop\` for that case |
| \`frame\` | \`{ time, value, state }\` | Fires on every frame that moved. \`useCropperFrame\` wraps it |
| \`stop\` | \`nothing\` | Fires when \`stop()\` ends all motion |
| \`destroy\` | \`nothing\` | Fires once, when \`destroy()\` runs |

A listener that throws stops nothing. The other listeners still run, the crop keeps working, and the error comes back as an uncaught error on a microtask.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes exporting as markdown 1`] = `
"# Exporting

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Turn the crop into an image file in the browser, or send the crop value to your server and cut the original there.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/exporting runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, a track of JPEG, PNG and WebP, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Save the crop as a file

\`exportCrop\` comes from \`@zriz/core/dom\`. \`@zriz/react\` needs \`@zriz/core\` as a peer dependency and re-exports the types an editor needs, such as \`CropValue\`, \`Cropper\` and \`ChangeReason\`. The types of \`exportCrop\` and \`loadImage\`, such as \`ExportOptions\`, \`ExportResult\`, \`LoadedImage\` and \`ZrizErrorCode\`, come from \`@zriz/core/dom\` too. Pure functions and the other core types, such as \`parse\` and \`ExportPlan\`, come from \`@zriz/core\`. [Quick start](https://zriz.dev/docs/quick-start.md) installs it. Without React, add it alone:

\`\`\`sh
pnpm add @zriz/core
\`\`\`

Keep it at the version \`@zriz/react\` asks for. A second copy of \`@zriz/core\` cannot see the photo, see \`no-image\` on [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md).

Pass a ref to \`actionsRef\` on \`Root\`, and pass \`cropper.current\` to \`exportCrop\` from a save button. The button stays disabled until \`onLoad\` fires for this file, because an export before the photo loads rejects with \`no-image\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { exportCrop } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

export const SaveCrop = ({ file }: { file: File }) => {
  const cropper = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const running = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);
  const [loaded, setLoaded] = useState<File | null>(null);
  const [saving, setSaving] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => () => running.current?.abort(), [file]);

  const save = async () => {
    if (!cropper.current || running.current) return;
    const controller = new AbortController();

    running.current = controller;
    setSaving(true);
    const result = await exportCrop(cropper.current, {
      type: "image/webp",
      maxSize: { width: 2048, height: 2048 },
      signal: controller.signal,
    }).catch((error: unknown) => {
      if (!controller.signal.aborted) console.error(error);

      return null;
    });

    running.current = null;
    setSaving(false);
    if (!result) return;
    const { blob, type } = result;
    const link = document.createElement("a");

    link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    const extension = type === "image/webp" ? "webp" : "png";

    link.download = \`crop.\${extension}\`;
    link.click();
    setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(link.href), 1000);
  };

  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.Root
        src={file}
        actionsRef={cropper}
        onLoad={() => setLoaded(file)}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport style={{ width: 480, height: 360 }}>
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button
        type="button"
        disabled={loaded !== file}
        aria-disabled={saving}
        onClick={save}
      >
        save
      </button>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

An export can fail on a very large photo, so the snippet catches it. [Errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists the codes. \`running\` holds the export in flight. A second click does nothing, and a new file aborts it, so an old crop never downloads after the photo changed.

The demo above runs this export with the same \`maxSize\`, in the format you pick in its track, and uses the styles from the zriz site.

The export draws from the original, not the preview on screen. Without \`maxSize\`, the file has the crop's size in source pixels.

## Export a round avatar

With \`shape="circle"\` on \`Root\`, \`exportCrop\` reads the shape and returns a PNG with transparent corners. \`exportImage(cropper, image)\` exports a \`LoadedImage\` from \`loadImage\` instead. It does not read the shape, so pass \`shape: "circle"\` yourself. [Avatar](https://zriz.dev/docs/avatar.md) has the whole component.

## Choose size and format

- \`type\` \`"image/jpeg" | "image/png" | "image/webp"\` = \`"image/jpeg"\`

  The file format. A circle crop defaults to PNG. JPEG has no transparency, so clear pixels come out white
- \`quality\` \`number\` = \`0.9\`

  JPEG and WebP quality, from 0 to 1
- \`maxSize\` \`{ width, height }\`

  Scales the file down to fit inside this box, never up. A box with a side that is not a positive number is ignored
- \`colorSpace\` \`"srgb" | "display-p3"\` = \`"srgb"\`

  \`"display-p3"\` keeps wide colour when the photo came from a file or URL. It decodes on the main thread
- \`shape\` \`"rect" | "circle"\`

  \`"rect"\` cuts a rectangle. \`"circle"\` cuts a circle, with transparent corners in PNG and WebP and white ones in JPEG. Defaults to \`shape\` on \`Root\`
- \`signal\` \`AbortSignal\`

  Cancels a running export, which then rejects with \`aborted\`

\`exportCrop\` resolves to \`{ blob, type, width, height, scaled, crop }\`. Read \`type\` before you name the file, because a browser that cannot encode the type you asked for returns PNG. \`scaled\` is \`true\` when the file came out smaller than the crop. Three things cause it: \`maxSize\`, the export size limit in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md), and the retry at half and a quarter of the size when the browser cannot draw or encode the file. \`crop\` is the export plan the file was drawn from, at the scale it came out.

Exports run one at a time, in the order you call them. An aborted export stops waiting at once, but one the browser already started holds the next one back until it ends.

The browser draws pixels onto a canvas and encodes them, so EXIF, GPS, XMP, thumbnails, gain maps and bytes after the image never reach the file, and neither does 16-bit depth. Safari writes a small EXIF block of its own, with only the crop's size and colour space. [Security](https://zriz.dev/docs/security.md) covers the original, which keeps all of it.

## Export an exact size

\`maxSize\` never scales up, so a crop smaller than the box comes out smaller. Set \`minSize\` on \`Root\` to the same size, and every crop is at least that big in source pixels. On a photo at least that big, a square crop with \`minSize\` and \`maxSize\` both at 512 by 512 exports at 512 by 512.

\`minSize\` reads along the photo's own sides, so after a quarter turn a 1200 by 630 minimum can export at 630 by 1200. While you straighten a crop of another shape than the minimum, covering the photo comes first, so that crop can drop below it. The angle stays free while the minimum's diagonal fits along each side of the photo, so only the minimum's size next to the photo counts. A bigger minimum lowers \`getRotationLimit()\` below 45, and on a square photo that starts at a square minimum of about 71% of its side. The crop does not grow back on its own.

## Handle a failed export

\`exportCrop\` rejects with a \`ZrizError\`. [Errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists each code an export can fail with, when it fires and how to fix it.

After the first photo appears, zriz loads the export code in the background, so the first export does not wait for a download.

## Show a small preview of the crop

Call \`exportCrop\` with a small \`maxSize\` in \`onLoad\` and in \`onValueCommit\`, and show the blob with \`URL.createObjectURL()\`. The first photo sends no commit until someone touches it, so \`onLoad\` draws the first preview. A later photo commits only when it has to refit the crop. Pass an \`AbortController\` \`signal\` and abort the last export on each commit, so a slow one never overwrites a newer one. Revoke the old object URL when a new preview replaces it:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { exportCrop } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

export const CropWithPreview = ({ file }: { file: File }) => {
  const cropper = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const last = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);
  const [preview, setPreview] = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => () => last.current?.abort(), []);

  useEffect(
    () => () => {
      if (preview) URL.revokeObjectURL(preview);
    },
    [preview]
  );

  const refresh = async () => {
    if (!cropper.current) return;
    last.current?.abort();
    const controller = new AbortController();

    last.current = controller;

    try {
      const { blob } = await exportCrop(cropper.current, {
        maxSize: { width: 256, height: 256 },
        signal: controller.signal,
      });

      if (!controller.signal.aborted) {
        setPreview(URL.createObjectURL(blob));
      }
    } catch (error) {
      if (!controller.signal.aborted) console.error(error);
    }
  };

  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.Root
        src={file}
        actionsRef={cropper}
        onLoad={() => void refresh()}
        onValueCommit={() => void refresh()}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport
          style={{ maxWidth: 480, height: 360 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      {preview && <img src={preview} alt="Crop preview" />}
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

## Cut the original on a server

To cut on a server, send the crop value from \`getValue()\` and let the server recompute the plan, as in [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md). Never trust a plan a client sends. \`getPixelCrop()\` returns the export plan in source pixels, or \`null\` until a photo loads, and each commit carries it as \`details.pixelCrop\`. Use it for your own tools, and read all of it, because \`pixelRect\` is \`null\` with a straighten angle or perspective.

- \`width\` \`number\`

  The output width in pixels, never below 1 px. \`getPixelCrop()\` takes a scale, 1 by default and for anything but a positive number or a scale that pushes a side past \`Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER\`
- \`height\` \`number\`

  The output height in pixels at the same scale
- \`pixelRect\` \`{ left, top, width, height } | null\`

  \`{ left, top, width, height }\` in the upright original, or \`null\` with a straighten angle or perspective
- \`rotateExpand\` \`{ flop, degrees, dx, dy }\`

  The mirror as \`flop\`, the quarter turns times 90 plus the straighten angle as \`degrees\`, and the crop's centre from the photo's centre after that whole turn as \`dx\` and \`dy\`, in source pixels
- \`forward\` \`[a, b, c, d, e, f]\`

  The map from source pixels to output pixels, as \`[a, b, c, d, e, f]\` in canvas \`setTransform()\` order. Alone it holds only when \`homography\` is \`null\`. With perspective, \`output = forward(c + H(s - c))\`, with \`c\` the centre of the image in source pixels
- \`inverse\` \`[a, b, c, d, e, f]\`

  The map from output pixels back to source pixels. Alone it holds only when \`homography\` is \`null\`. With perspective, the source pixel is \`s = c + H⁻¹(inverse(output) - c)\`, with \`c\` as above
- \`homography\` \`number[] | null\`

  The 3 by 3 perspective matrix \`H\`, as 9 numbers row by row, or \`null\` with no perspective. Divide by the third coordinate after you apply it

With no straighten and no perspective, the plan comes down to plain numbers that any tool can use: the rectangle, the flop and the turn. \`pixelRect\` is the truth for the file: its edges land on whole source pixels, so on a very small photo the frame on screen can sit up to half a source pixel from the file edge. A side never goes below 1 px, so a thin crop can come out slightly over \`maxSize\`.

\`toSharp(plan, image)\` from \`@zriz/core\` takes the plan and the upright photo's size and returns sharp steps with \`kind\` \`"extract"\` or \`"affine"\`. It ignores perspective without an error, so check \`plan.homography\` first. [Server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) shows the route for a level crop and a straightened one.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes faq as markdown 1`] = `
"# FAQ

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Short answers, each with a link to the page that covers it in full.

## Setup

### What does zriz need

React 18.2 or newer, 19 included, from one build. Both packages ship types, and \`@types/react\` 18.2.6 or newer is an optional peer. [Quick start](https://zriz.dev/docs/quick-start.md) has the install.

### Which browsers does it run in

Chrome, Edge and Firefox in their latest two versions, Safari and iOS Safari 18 and newer, and Chrome and Samsung Internet on Android. A two-finger trackpad twist rotates only in Safari. On a trackpad in other browsers, rotate with the dial and the keys. Two fingers on a touch screen rotate in every browser.

### Does it work without a bundler

Yes, with an import map. Map \`react\`, \`react/jsx-runtime\`, \`@zriz/react\`, \`@zriz/react/cropper\`, \`@zriz/core\` and \`@zriz/core/dom\`. The parts also import \`@zriz/react/warn\`, so point that at \`@zriz/react/dist/warn-off.js\`.

To keep the warnings during development, point it at \`dist/warn.js\` and run \`window.process = { env: {} }\` before the modules load. Without a \`process\` global, \`dist/warn.js\` stays silent too.

### Does it work with the Next.js app router

Yes. Every part is a client component, so a server component can render them. Callbacks such as \`onValueCommit\` are functions, so put them in a client file of your own.

### Does it render on the server

Yes. The server renders the parts with no photo, and the photo loads in the browser after hydration.

### Does it work with Vue or Svelte

There are no Vue or Svelte parts. \`@zriz/core/dom\` binds a cropper to plain elements, so any framework can drive it. [Vanilla JS](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md) shows the setup.

### Can I use my own button component

Yes. Pass it to \`render\` on any part, and zriz merges its props and ref into yours. [Composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md) has the code.

### Why is my viewport empty

\`Viewport\` has no height of its own, so it starts at zero height. Give it a height, as in [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md).

### Can I test it with jsdom

jsdom has no \`ResizeObserver\`, so \`Viewport\`, \`Image\` and \`DialTicks\` throw when they mount. Stub it in your test setup. To test crop logic with no DOM at all, use \`createCropper\` with a manual clock, as [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md) shows.

\`\`\`ts
globalThis.ResizeObserver ??= class {
  observe() {}
  unobserve() {}
  disconnect() {}
};
\`\`\`

### Why do Node and Jest fail on the stylesheet

Node cannot import a \`.css\` file, and Jest cannot either until you map it. Point \`\\.css$\` at an empty module with \`moduleNameMapper\` in your Jest config. Vitest and Bun load the import as it is, and in an app your bundler handles it.

## Photos and export

### Can it open HEIC photos from an iPhone

Safari decodes HEIC, so iPhone photos open there. In other browsers the load fails with \`unsupported-format\`. To open them everywhere, call \`loadImage\` yourself with a HEIC decoder as \`decode\`, as [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) shows.

### How do I get the cropped file

Call \`exportCrop\` from \`@zriz/core/dom\` with the cropper from \`actionsRef\`. It resolves with an object that holds the file in \`blob\`, next to its \`width\` and \`height\`. [Exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md) has the code.

### Can I put it in a dialog or a drawer

Yes. A native \`<dialog>\` does not lock the page, so a wheel at the pan limit or over the dial scrolls what is behind it. \`html:has(dialog[open]) { overflow: hidden; }\` holds the page. Keep \`Announcer\` inside the dialog, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md).

Radix \`forceMount\` with CSS hiding keeps \`aria-hidden\` on your app and the scroll lock while closed, so mount the dialog only when it is open. Base UI \`keepMounted\` leaves the page clean.

Vaul starts with focus on the page, and the first presses of tab land behind the drawer. Pass \`autoFocus\` to \`Drawer.Root\`. [Viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md) and [root](https://zriz.dev/docs/root.md) have the layout and swipe notes.

### Can I crop on the server

Yes. Send the original file and the value from \`getValue()\`, recompute the plan on the server, then turn that plan into sharp calls with \`toSharp\`. [Server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) has the route.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes full-editor as markdown 1`] = `
"# Full editor

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

The crop area, the dial, zoom, aspect ratios, the buttons and a JPEG export in one component.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/full-editor runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, the straighten dial with rotate and flip, the aspect ratio track, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Lay out the crop area

Give the viewport a size. The stylesheet paints the mask, the crop frame, the handles and the grid. Eight \`Handle\` parts let the visitor drag every corner and edge.

\`\`\`tsx
const handles = [
  "top-left",
  "top",
  "top-right",
  "right",
  "bottom-right",
  "bottom",
  "bottom-left",
  "left",
] as const;

<Cropper.Viewport
  style={{ maxWidth: 640, height: 480, padding: 24 }}
>
  <Cropper.Image />
  <Cropper.Mask />
  <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
    <Cropper.Grid />
    {handles.map((position) => (
      <Cropper.Handle
        key={position}
        position={position}
      />
    ))}
  </Cropper.Area>
</Cropper.Viewport>
\`\`\`

## Straighten with the dial

The dial fills its parent unless you give it a width, see [dial](https://zriz.dev/docs/dial.md).

\`DialValue\` prints the angle as a whole number. \`format\` adds the degree sign.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.DialValue
  format={(degrees) =>
    \`\${Math.sign(degrees) * Math.round(Math.abs(degrees))}°\`
  }
/>
<Cropper.Dial
  aria-label="Straighten"
  style={{ maxWidth: 361 }}
>
  <Cropper.DialTicks />
</Cropper.Dial>
\`\`\`

## Add a zoom slider

zriz ships \`Zoom\` and \`ZoomThumb\` with no paint. \`track\` draws a 2 px line and \`thumb\` draws the knob. zriz moves the knob from the left end at 1× to the right end at the largest zoom.

\`\`\`tsx
const track = {
  width: 200,
  height: 24,
  backgroundImage:
    "linear-gradient(currentColor, currentColor)",
  backgroundSize: "100% 2px",
  backgroundPosition: "center",
  backgroundRepeat: "no-repeat",
};

const thumb = {
  top: 4,
  width: 16,
  height: 16,
  borderRadius: 8,
  background: "currentColor",
  translate: "-50%",
};

<Cropper.Zoom aria-label="Zoom" style={track}>
  <Cropper.ZoomThumb style={thumb} />
</Cropper.Zoom>
\`\`\`

Forced colours mode removes a gradient and a plain background, so the slider would vanish. Save this as \`forced-colors.css\` next to the editor, which imports it. It brings both back in \`CanvasText\`. The styles above are inline, so it needs \`!important\`:

\`\`\`css
@media (forced-colors: active) {
  [data-zriz-item="zoom"] {
    forced-color-adjust: none;
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      CanvasText,
      CanvasText
    ) !important;
  }
  [data-zriz-item="zoom-thumb"] {
    background: CanvasText !important;
  }
}
\`\`\`

## Pick an aspect ratio

\`AspectGroup\` is one tab stop. [Aspect ratio](https://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio.md) lists its keys.

\`Orientation\` swaps 4:5 for 5:4 and 16:9 for 9:16. It is off while the aspect is free, original or square. After a swap no item is checked, because the list has neither 5:4 nor 9:16. [Aspect ratio](https://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio.md) flips the labels with the orientation so one item stays checked.

\`\`\`tsx
const aspects = [
  { label: "free", value: "free" },
  { label: "original", value: "original" },
  { label: "square", value: 1 },
  { label: "4:5", value: 4 / 5 },
  { label: "16:9", value: 16 / 9 },
] as const;

<Cropper.AspectGroup aria-label="Aspect ratio">
  {aspects.map((aspect) => (
    <Cropper.AspectItem
      key={aspect.label}
      value={aspect.value}
    >
      {aspect.label}
    </Cropper.AspectItem>
  ))}
</Cropper.AspectGroup>
<Cropper.Orientation>orientation</Cropper.Orientation>
\`\`\`

## Turn, flip and reset

The buttons come with no paint, so put your own icons or words inside. \`Rotate\` turns the photo 90° counter-clockwise, \`Flip\` mirrors it, and \`Reset\` puts the crop back where it started. \`Reset\` stays hidden until the crop changes.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Rotate>rotate</Cropper.Rotate>
<Cropper.Flip>flip</Cropper.Flip>
<Cropper.Reset>reset</Cropper.Reset>
\`\`\`

## Describe it to screen readers

\`Instructions\` is hidden text. The crop area points to it with \`aria-describedby\`, so a screen reader reads it when the area gets focus.

The dial and the zoom slider read out their own values. The buttons do not, so \`announce\` speaks for them after each commit. It returns \`null\` for any other change. [Accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) covers the keys and the labels.

\`\`\`tsx
const spoken: Partial<Record<ChangeReason, string>> = {
  "rotate-90": "Turned 90 degrees",
  flip: "Flipped",
  orientation: "Orientation swapped",
  reset: "Crop reset",
};

const announce = (_: CropValue, details: CommitDetails) =>
  spoken[details.reason] ?? null;

const instructions = [
  "Drag the photo to move it.",
  "Arrows move the crop. Alt and arrows resize it.",
  "Plus and minus zoom, and 0 resets.",
].join(" ");

<Cropper.Instructions>
  {instructions}
</Cropper.Instructions>
<Cropper.Announcer announce={announce} />
\`\`\`

## Export a JPEG

\`exportCrop\` makes a JPEG unless the crop is a circle. The button stays off until \`onLoad\` fires, because an export before that rejects. \`exportCrop\` comes from \`@zriz/core/dom\`.

An export can still fail on a very large photo, so \`save\` shows its own alert when it does. \`onError\` shows a second one when the photo does not open. [Errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists the codes.

\`key={src}\` gives each photo a fresh cropper, and the check on \`shown\` turns the button off and hides both alerts when \`src\` changes.

\`running\` holds the export in flight. A second click does nothing, and the button keeps focus because \`saving\` sets \`aria-disabled\` and leaves it enabled. A new \`src\` aborts the export, so an old crop never reaches \`onSave\` after the photo changed.

\`\`\`tsx
const running = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);

const [saving, setSaving] = useState(false);

const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);
const [exportFailed, setExportFailed] = useState(false);
const [shown, setShown] = useState(src);

if (shown !== src) {
  setShown(src);
  setReady(false);
  setFailed(false);
  setExportFailed(false);
}

useEffect(() => () => running.current?.abort(), [src]);

const save = async () => {
  if (!actions.current || running.current) return;
  const controller = new AbortController();

  running.current = controller;
  setSaving(true);
  setExportFailed(false);

  try {
    const { blob } = await exportCrop(actions.current, {
      signal: controller.signal,
    });

    onSave(blob);
  } catch {
    setExportFailed(!controller.signal.aborted);
  } finally {
    running.current = null;
    setSaving(false);
  }
};

<Cropper.Root
  key={src}
  actionsRef={actions}
  src={src}
  onLoad={() => setReady(true)}
  onError={() => setFailed(true)}
>

<button
  type="button"
  disabled={!ready}
  aria-disabled={saving}
  onClick={save}
>
  export
</button>

{failed && <p role="alert">this photo does not open.</p>}

{exportFailed && (
  <p role="alert">this crop did not save. try again.</p>
)}
\`\`\`

## The whole file

\`Editor\` takes a photo URL and hands \`onSave\` the JPEG:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { exportCrop } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type {
  ChangeReason,
  CommitDetails,
  Cropper as CropperApi,
  CropValue,
} from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import "./forced-colors.css";

const handles = [
  "top-left",
  "top",
  "top-right",
  "right",
  "bottom-right",
  "bottom",
  "bottom-left",
  "left",
] as const;

const track = {
  width: 200,
  height: 24,
  backgroundImage:
    "linear-gradient(currentColor, currentColor)",
  backgroundSize: "100% 2px",
  backgroundPosition: "center",
  backgroundRepeat: "no-repeat",
};

const thumb = {
  top: 4,
  width: 16,
  height: 16,
  borderRadius: 8,
  background: "currentColor",
  translate: "-50%",
};

const aspects = [
  { label: "free", value: "free" },
  { label: "original", value: "original" },
  { label: "square", value: 1 },
  { label: "4:5", value: 4 / 5 },
  { label: "16:9", value: 16 / 9 },
] as const;

const spoken: Partial<Record<ChangeReason, string>> = {
  "rotate-90": "Turned 90 degrees",
  flip: "Flipped",
  orientation: "Orientation swapped",
  reset: "Crop reset",
};

const announce = (_: CropValue, details: CommitDetails) =>
  spoken[details.reason] ?? null;

const instructions = [
  "Drag the photo to move it.",
  "Arrows move the crop. Alt and arrows resize it.",
  "Plus and minus zoom, and 0 resets.",
].join(" ");

type Props = { src: string; onSave: (jpeg: Blob) => void };

export const Editor = ({ src, onSave }: Props) => {
  const actions = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const running = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);
  const [saving, setSaving] = useState(false);
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);
  const [exportFailed, setExportFailed] = useState(false);
  const [shown, setShown] = useState(src);

  if (shown !== src) {
    setShown(src);
    setReady(false);
    setFailed(false);
    setExportFailed(false);
  }

  useEffect(() => () => running.current?.abort(), [src]);

  const save = async () => {
    if (!actions.current || running.current) return;
    const controller = new AbortController();

    running.current = controller;
    setSaving(true);
    setExportFailed(false);

    try {
      const { blob } = await exportCrop(actions.current, {
        signal: controller.signal,
      });

      onSave(blob);
    } catch {
      setExportFailed(!controller.signal.aborted);
    } finally {
      running.current = null;
      setSaving(false);
    }
  };

  return (
    <Cropper.Root
      key={src}
      actionsRef={actions}
      src={src}
      onLoad={() => setReady(true)}
      onError={() => setFailed(true)}
    >
      <Cropper.Viewport
        style={{ maxWidth: 640, height: 480, padding: 24 }}
      >
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
          <Cropper.Grid />
          {handles.map((position) => (
            <Cropper.Handle
              key={position}
              position={position}
            />
          ))}
        </Cropper.Area>
      </Cropper.Viewport>
      <Cropper.DialValue
        format={(degrees) =>
          \`\${Math.sign(degrees) * Math.round(Math.abs(degrees))}°\`
        }
      />
      <Cropper.Dial
        aria-label="Straighten"
        style={{ maxWidth: 361 }}
      >
        <Cropper.DialTicks />
      </Cropper.Dial>
      <Cropper.Zoom aria-label="Zoom" style={track}>
        <Cropper.ZoomThumb style={thumb} />
      </Cropper.Zoom>
      <Cropper.AspectGroup aria-label="Aspect ratio">
        {aspects.map((aspect) => (
          <Cropper.AspectItem
            key={aspect.label}
            value={aspect.value}
          >
            {aspect.label}
          </Cropper.AspectItem>
        ))}
      </Cropper.AspectGroup>
      <Cropper.Orientation>orientation</Cropper.Orientation>
      <Cropper.Rotate>rotate</Cropper.Rotate>
      <Cropper.Flip>flip</Cropper.Flip>
      <Cropper.Reset>reset</Cropper.Reset>
      <Cropper.Instructions>
        {instructions}
      </Cropper.Instructions>
      <Cropper.Announcer announce={announce} />
      <button
        type="button"
        disabled={!ready}
        aria-disabled={saving}
        onClick={save}
      >
        export
      </button>
      {failed && <p role="alert">this photo does not open.</p>}
      {exportFailed && (
        <p role="alert">this crop did not save. try again.</p>
      )}
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`

The demo above runs this file with the icons and styles from the zriz site. It shows the angle to one decimal and calls square 1:1. Its aspect labels turn with the orientation, as on [aspect ratio](https://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio.md). It leaves out the zoom slider, and wheel and pinch still zoom.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes hooks as markdown 1`] = `
"# Hooks

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Seven hooks from \`@zriz/react\` that read the cropper and build parts of your own.

Call a hook in any component inside \`Root\`. This label reads \`edited\` once the crop changes:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperState } from "@zriz/react";

export const Status = () => {
  const pristine = useCropperState((s) => s.pristine);

  return <span>{pristine ? "original" : "edited"}</span>;
};
\`\`\`

Outside \`Root\`, pass the cropper yourself. It goes last in \`useCropperState\`, \`useCropperValue\` and \`useCropperFrame\`, and in the options of \`useZoom\`. Without it, they throw the error listed on [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md).

## Call methods from your own buttons

Each \`Root\` runs one cropper. Pass a ref to \`actionsRef\` to reach it once \`Root\` has mounted. The ref stays set while a suspense boundary shows its fallback, and is \`null\` while \`<Activity>\` hides \`Root\`. When \`Root\` unmounts, the ref clears with the passive effects, so a parent's \`useLayoutEffect\` in that commit still reads the cropper. On a keyed remount, the old \`Root\` lets go after the new one takes the ref. A ref object or a stable callback with no cleanup keeps the new cropper. An inline callback gets \`null\` last, and a cleanup your callback returns runs last, so neither suits a keyed \`Root\`. [Cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md) lists its methods.

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useRef } from "react";

export const Editor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const cropper = useRef<CropperApi>(null);

  const save = () => {
    console.log(cropper.current?.getPixelCrop());
  };

  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.Root src={src} actionsRef={cropper}>
        <Cropper.Viewport
          style={{ maxWidth: 480, height: 360 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button onClick={save}>save</button>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

## The hooks

### \`useCropper\`

\`useCropper(options?)\` creates the cropper in a component above \`Root\`. Use it when that component needs the cropper during render. Hand the cropper to \`Root\` and to any control:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { attachImage } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { useCropper } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useEffect } from "react";

export const Editor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const cropper = useCropper({ maxZoom: 8 });

  useEffect(() => () => attachImage(cropper, null), [cropper]);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => cropper.reset()}>reset</button>
      <Cropper.Root cropper={cropper} src={src}>
        <Cropper.Viewport
          style={{ maxWidth: 480, height: 360 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

It takes the \`createCropper\` options on [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md) except \`clock\`, plus the controlled \`aspect\`, \`rotation\` and \`zoom\`, and \`cropper\` to reuse one you already made. \`disabled\`, \`maxZoom\`, \`minSize\`, \`value\` and the two callbacks follow later renders. The cropper reads \`defaultValue\`, \`image\`, \`focalLength\` and \`plugins\` once, when it is made.

When \`Root\` gets \`cropper\`, it ignores its own \`defaultValue\`, \`image\`, \`focalLength\` and \`plugins\`, because the cropper already exists. Give each option to \`useCropper\` or to \`Root\`, never to both.

\`Root\` frees the photo only for a cropper it made. Free a \`useCropper\` cropper's photo yourself when the component unmounts, with \`attachImage(cropper, null)\` from \`@zriz/core/dom\`, as the snippet does. Add \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md).

\`Root\` keeps its photo while \`<Activity>\` hides it. React runs no cleanup when it removes a tree that is hidden, so \`Root\` cannot free the photo then. To remove a hidden \`Root\`, make the cropper with \`useCropper\` above it and call \`attachImage(cropper, null)\` yourself.

Pass one cropper to one \`Root\`. \`shape\`, \`motion\` and \`recenterDelay\` belong to the cropper, so a second \`Root\` overwrites them, even with its defaults. Keep \`Instructions\` in the \`Root\` that holds \`Area\`. Your own parts and hooks can reach the cropper through \`CropperContext\` or the \`cropper\` argument.

### \`useCropperState\`

\`useCropperState(selector, cropper?)\` returns the part of the snapshot you select and renders again when it changes. The snapshot changes when a state flips, never on every frame.

- \`ready\` \`boolean\`

  Is \`true\` once the cropper knows the photo's size. The \`image\` option makes it \`true\` before the file loads, so wait for \`onLoad\` before you export
- \`interacting\` \`ChangeReason | null\`

  Names the gesture in progress
- \`settling\` \`boolean\`

  Is \`true\` while any animation runs: a thrown dial, the recentre, a rotate or flip
- \`pristine\` \`boolean\`

  Is \`true\` while the crop matches the reset value
- \`disabled\` \`boolean\`

  Is \`true\` while the cropper refuses gestures and most writes, see [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md)
- \`aspect\` \`Aspect\`

  Holds the current aspect
- \`quarterTurns\` \`0 | 1 | 2 | 3\`

  Holds the current quarter turns
- \`mirrored\` \`boolean\`

  Is \`true\` while the photo is flipped
- \`committed\` \`CropValue\`

  Holds the last committed value

Return one field or a primitive. A selector that builds a new object or array on every call makes React render forever.

### \`useCropperValue\`

\`useCropperValue(cropper?)\` returns the last committed value and renders once per commit. A new \`value\` prop and writes with \`emit: false\` change it without a commit, so the hook does not render for them. The first fit when a photo loads does not commit either, but the hook renders when a photo arrives or leaves, so it shows that fit. The fields are on [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md).

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperValue } from "@zriz/react";

export const Angle = () => {
  const value = useCropperValue();

  return <p>{value.rotation.toFixed(1)}°</p>;
};
\`\`\`

### \`useCropperFrame\`

\`useCropperFrame(listener, cropper?)\` calls the listener on every frame that moved, without a render. The frame holds \`time\`, \`value\` and \`state\`, the crop in image pixels, which is \`null\` before a photo loads. [Cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md) lists its fields. Write the live angle straight into the DOM:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperFrame } from "@zriz/react";
import { useRef } from "react";

export const LiveAngle = () => {
  const label = useRef<HTMLOutputElement>(null);

  useCropperFrame(({ value }) => {
    const angle = value.rotation.toFixed(1);

    if (label.current) label.current.textContent = \`\${angle}°\`;
  });

  return <output ref={label} />;
};
\`\`\`

### \`useZoom\`

\`useZoom(options?)\` connects a slider of your own to the zoom. Each drag counts as one \`zoom-control\` gesture on [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md). It takes two options, \`max\` and \`cropper\`. [Zoom](https://zriz.dev/docs/zoom.md) builds a range input with it. It returns:

- \`value\` \`number\`

  Holds the zoom rounded to 3 decimals. It updates every frame
- \`min\` \`number\`

  Is always 1, the largest crop
- \`max\` \`number\`

  Is the \`max\` option, or the cropper's \`maxZoom\`, 15 by default. \`max\` sets the slider's range only. The cropper still stops at \`maxZoom\`
- \`dragging\` \`boolean\`

  Is \`true\` while the cropper has a \`zoom-control\` gesture open, so every \`Zoom\` and every \`useZoom\` on one cropper reads the same. A press on \`Zoom\` holds it until release. A \`useZoom\` change holds it until the last held pointer or key lifts, or, with nothing held, 150 ms after the last change
- \`onValueChange\` \`(zoom) => void\`

  Starts the gesture on the first call and sets the zoom. With no pointer or key held, the gesture ends 150 ms after the last call
- \`onValueCommitted\` \`() => void\`

  Ends the gesture. Call it when the slider lets go. The hook also ends it when the last held pointer or key lifts anywhere in the window, when the window loses focus and on unmount

### \`useCoverScale\`

\`useCoverScale({ image, crop, rotation })\` returns the smallest scale that keeps a straightened photo free of empty corners, for a crop centred on it. It calls no React hooks, so it is safe in loops and conditions. Outside React, \`coverScale(image, crop, radians)\` from \`@zriz/core\`, installed as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md), does the same.

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCoverScale } from "@zriz/react";

export const Scale = () => {
  const scale = useCoverScale({
    image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
    crop: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
    rotation: 5,
  });

  return <p>{scale.toFixed(4)}</p>;
};
\`\`\`

A full-frame crop of a 4032 × 3024 photo, straightened 5°, needs 1.1124. The photo grows 11.2%.

### \`useRender\`

\`useRender(options)\` renders an element the way every zriz part does. Use it to build a part of your own. This is the label from the top of the page as a part:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useCropperState, useRender } from "@zriz/react";
import type { ClassName, RenderProp } from "@zriz/react";

type EditedState = {
  pristine: boolean;
};

type EditedProps = {
  render?: RenderProp<EditedState>;
  className?: ClassName<EditedState>;
};

export const Edited = ({ render, className }: EditedProps) => {
  const pristine = useCropperState((s) => s.pristine);

  return useRender({
    tag: "span",
    state: { pristine },
    render,
    className,
    props: { children: pristine ? "original" : "edited" },
  });
};
\`\`\`

Your part follows the \`render\` and \`className\` rules on [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). It takes:

- \`tag\` \`keyof JSX.IntrinsicElements\` required

  Sets the tag, for example \`"span"\`
- \`state\` \`S\` required

  Goes to \`render\` and \`className\` functions
- \`props\` \`object\` required

  Sets the element's props. Take \`render\` and your own props out first, as the parts do
- \`ref\` \`Ref<Element>\`

  Attaches to the element
- \`render\` \`RenderProp<S>\`

  Replaces the element, as on every part
- \`className\` \`ClassName<S>\`

  Sets the class: a string, or a function of state
"
`;

exports[`content > writes introduction as markdown 1`] = `
"# Introduction

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

zriz is an image cropper for React with a straighten dial, like the crop tool in iOS Photos.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/introduction runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, the straighten dial with rotate and flip, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## What it does

| Feature | What you get |
| - | - |
| [Straighten](https://zriz.dev/docs/dial.md) | A dial turns the photo up to 45° each way and glides on after a flick. The photo zooms in by the least amount that keeps every corner of the crop filled |
| [Move and zoom](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md) | Drag, pinch, trackpad and wheel. Pull past an edge and the photo stretches, then springs back when you let go |
| [Rotate and flip](https://zriz.dev/docs/buttons.md) | Animated quarter turns and mirroring, and a reset that undoes every change |
| [Aspect ratio](https://zriz.dev/docs/aspect-ratio.md) | Free, the photo's own ratio or any ratio you name, with a portrait and landscape swap |
| [Circle crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/avatar.md) | A round mask for avatars, exported as a PNG with transparent corners |
| [Export](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md) | A file made off the main thread, or a plan your server uses to cut the original with sharp |
| [Accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) | Keys on every control, labels and announcements in your own words, and shorter motion when the system asks |
| [Styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) | An optional stylesheet, CSS variables, a data attribute for every state, dark mode and Tailwind |
| [Plain data](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md) | The crop is a small object you can save, restore, undo or send |
| [Without React](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md) | The same core binds to plain elements, so it runs in any framework |

## Build it from parts

Import the parts as one namespace from \`@zriz/react/cropper\`, and the hooks from \`@zriz/react\`. These pages call the parts by their short names, such as \`Root\`.

Each part renders one outer element, which takes your props, \`className\` and ref. \`Image\`, \`Mask\` and \`Area\` go inside \`Viewport\`. The dial, zoom and buttons can sit anywhere inside \`Root\`, so your layout decides where they go:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={photo} onValueCommit={save}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ maxWidth: 400, height: 300 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
  <Cropper.DialValue />
  <Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten">
    <Cropper.DialTicks />
  </Cropper.Dial>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

\`onValueCommit\` hands you the crop each time a change ends.

The demo above runs this code. It adds handles, a grid, rotate, flip and reset, shows the angle to one decimal and uses the styles from the zriz site.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes loading-images as markdown 1`] = `
"# Loading images

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Open a photo from a file input, a URL or a blob, and check which formats and sizes load.

## Open a file from an input

\`src\` on \`Root\` takes a URL string, a \`Blob\`, a \`File\`, or \`null\` for an empty cropper. Keep the picked file in state and pass it straight to \`src\`:

\`\`\`tsx
<input
  aria-label="Choose a photo"
  type="file"
  accept="image/*"
  onChange={(event) =>
    setFile(event.target.files?.[0] ?? null)
  }
/>
<Cropper.Root src={file}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ maxWidth: 480, height: 360 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

zriz reads the format from the first 4096 bytes of the file, so a wrong name or a wrong raster mime type does not stop a photo from opening. A file declared as a raster image type never opens as SVG, and \`svg={false}\` on \`Root\` or \`svg: false\` on \`loadImage\` refuses SVG altogether. Both fail with \`decode\`. When \`src\` changes, zriz cancels a load that is still running and keeps \`onError\` quiet for it. The previous photo stays on screen until the new one is ready, and stays there when the new one fails.

## Start each photo from the full frame

A new \`src\` keeps the current crop, angle and flip, and fits them to the new photo. A controlled \`zoom\` goes back to the prop when the new photo has another size. To start every photo from the whole frame, give \`Root\` a \`key\` that changes with each pick, such as a counter you bump in \`onChange\`. A new \`key\` also frees the previous photo at once.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root key={picks} src={file}>
  {children}
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## Open a URL on another domain

zriz fetches a URL with \`fetch\` in CORS mode, so the server that hosts the photo must send \`Access-Control-Allow-Origin\`. Without it, the load fails with \`cross-origin\`. A 404 or any other error status fails with \`decode\`.

If that server needs cookies, pass \`crossOrigin="use-credentials"\`. The server then has to name your origin in \`Access-Control-Allow-Origin\`, not \`*\`, and send \`Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\`.

If that server wants a token in a header, fetch the photo yourself with that header and pass the response's blob to \`src\`.

## Check which formats open

| Format | What happens |
| - | - |
| JPEG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, GIF | Open in every current browser |
| BMP | Opens in every current browser, but Safari cannot open some BMP files with a v4 or v5 header. Those fail with \`decode\`. Pass a \`decode\` function to open them, or convert the file to JPEG first |
| HEIC, JPEG XL, TIFF | Open where the browser decodes them. They fail with \`unsupported-format\` elsewhere, and when the file is damaged |
| SVG | Takes its size from \`width\` and \`height\` in px, in, cm, mm, q, pt or pc, or from \`viewBox\`. With one side and a \`viewBox\`, the other follows the ratio. With no \`viewBox\`, a missing width opens at 300 and a missing height at 150. A side in \`%\` or \`em\` counts as missing. zriz draws it once at that size, at most the preview size, and exports from that drawing |
| Animated GIF, PNG, WebP, AVIF | Open on their first frame. From a file or URL, \`animated\` on the loaded image is \`true\`, and always for a GIF. A HEIC sequence also reports \`true\`. An \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\`, \`OffscreenCanvas\` or \`ImageBitmap\` source always reports \`false\`, even an \`img\` showing a GIF. An \`img\` already playing opens on the frame it shows in Safari, so pass a \`Blob\` for the first frame |

zriz walks the structure of a JPEG, PNG, AVIF or HEIC before it decodes, so a file cut short fails with \`decode\` in every browser. Data after the end of the file still opens. Firefox opens a cut GIF, and Firefox and Safari open a cut BMP, with the missing part painted in.

zriz measures the crop value and the export plan in the photo as the browser shows it. Every EXIF orientation in a JPEG opens upright in every browser. Chromium and Firefox ignore the orientation of a WebP and Safari honours it, so a tagged WebP opens differently per browser, and the route in [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) refuses it.

## Handle large photos

A worker decodes a preview sized for the screen. The original stays a blob until you export.

| Limit | Size |
| - | - |
| Preview size | Twice the screen's long edge in device pixels, at most 4096 px on a touch screen and 8192 px elsewhere. \`previewEdge\` on \`Root\` sets it |
| Export size | 16.8 megapixels on a touch screen and 268 megapixels elsewhere, with a long edge of at most 16383 px for WebP and 65500 px for other types. A bigger crop comes out scaled down |

Before it decodes, zriz reads the pixel count from the file's header. A JPEG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, HEIC, GIF, BMP or TIFF over \`maxPixels\` fails with \`too-large\`. A GIF counts its largest frame. A JPEG XL has no count zriz can read, so it fails after the decode, and so does what a \`decode\` function returns. The limit is 268402689 pixels, which opens every camera photo and matches sharp. Pass \`maxPixels\` to \`loadImage\` to change it. \`maxPixels\` does not apply to an \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\`, \`OffscreenCanvas\` or \`ImageBitmap\` source, or to an SVG, which zriz draws at most at the preview size.

A load has one time limit. A download stays inside it while bytes keep arriving, and the decode gets what is left after the last byte. Past it the load fails with \`timeout\`, and [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) gives the number. zriz runs six downloads and three decodes at a time, and a photo waiting for its turn is not on the clock. A URL that hangs holds one of the six until the limit. To allow more time, load the photo yourself and pass \`timeout\`, as in the HEIC section below.

A content security policy needs these sources:

| Directive | What it enables |
| - | - |
| \`worker-src blob:\` | Decoding and exporting off the main thread. Without it zriz does both on the main thread, and the page can stutter while a large photo opens |
| \`img-src blob:\` | The moving layer during gestures and display-p3 export. Without it the photo paints on the canvas during gestures, a crop with perspective stays empty, and a display-p3 export fails with \`decode\` |
| \`img-src data:\` | SVG files. Without it an SVG fails with \`decode\` |
| \`connect-src\` | Each host you load photos from, plus \`blob:\` and \`data:\` if you pass those URLs as \`src\`. Without it the load fails with \`cross-origin\` |

With \`require-trusted-types-for 'script'\`, add \`zriz\` to \`trusted-types\`. zriz starts its workers through a policy of that name, and the policy only takes \`blob:\` URLs. Without it the page works, but decodes and exports on the main thread.

A strict \`style-src\` also refuses \`style\` attributes in server HTML. The parts write their own styles again during hydration, so they work after it. A \`style\` prop of yours on a server rendered part stays refused. Add \`style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'\` to allow it, or give the part a class from your stylesheet instead.

## Open HEIC on every browser

To decode a format the browser cannot, call \`loadImage\` yourself and pass a \`decode\` function. zriz calls it only when the browser's own decode fails. It gets the \`Blob\` and returns an \`ImageBitmap\`. Save this as \`decoder.ts\`. Its \`convert\` sends the file to a route on your server that turns it into JPEG, so the original leaves the device. [Security](https://zriz.dev/docs/security.md) says how to treat it. \`loadImage\` and \`attachImage\` come from \`@zriz/core/dom\`, so add \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md):

\`\`\`ts
import { attachImage, loadImage } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import type { Cropper } from "@zriz/react";

const convert = async (blob: Blob) => {
  const response = await fetch("/api/convert", {
    method: "POST",
    body: blob,
  });

  return await createImageBitmap(await response.blob());
};

export const open = async (
  cropper: Cropper,
  file: File,
  signal: AbortSignal
) => {
  const image = await loadImage(file, {
    timeout: 30_000,
    decode: convert,
    signal,
  });

  attachImage(cropper, image);
};
\`\`\`

Pass a ref to \`actionsRef\`, leave \`src\` unset on \`Root\` and call \`open()\` from the file input. \`onLoad\` and \`onError\` do not fire for a photo you attach yourself, so the component tracks both from \`open()\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import { open } from "./decoder";

type Props = { onSave: (cropper: CropperApi) => void };

export const Heic = ({ onSave }: Props) => {
  const cropper = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const last = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => () => last.current?.abort(), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input
        aria-label="Choose a photo"
        type="file"
        accept="image/*,.heic"
        onChange={(event) => {
          const file = event.target.files?.[0];

          if (!file || !cropper.current) return;
          last.current?.abort();
          const controller = new AbortController();

          last.current = controller;
          setReady(false);
          setFailed(false);

          open(cropper.current, file, controller.signal).then(
            () => setReady(true),
            () => {
              if (!controller.signal.aborted) setFailed(true);
            }
          );
        }}
      />
      <Cropper.Root actionsRef={cropper}>
        <Cropper.Viewport
          style={{ maxWidth: 480, height: 360 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button
        type="button"
        disabled={!ready}
        onClick={() =>
          cropper.current && onSave(cropper.current)
        }
      >
        save
      </button>
      {failed && (
        <p role="alert">this photo does not open here.</p>
      )}
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`ready\` turns the save button on once the photo is attached. \`onSave\` gets the cropper for \`exportCrop\`, as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md). A new pick or an unmount aborts the load still running, so a slow file never replaces a newer one or lands on a cropper that left.

\`loadImage\` also takes \`timeout\` in milliseconds, \`previewEdge\`, the longest side of the preview in pixels, \`maxPixels\`, \`svg\`, \`crossOrigin\` and an abort \`signal\`. It accepts an \`img\`, \`video\`, \`canvas\`, \`OffscreenCanvas\` or \`ImageBitmap\` as its source. A \`video\` needs a current frame, so wait for \`loadeddata\` or \`seeked\`, or the load fails with \`decode\`. A fully transparent frame counts as no frame.

\`attachImage\` frees a photo when it leaves its last cropper, and \`destroy()\` does the same for the photo on that cropper. Attaching to a destroyed cropper frees the photo at once. A freed photo cannot be attached again, so load it again. A photo that never reached a cropper holds memory until you call \`discardImage(image)\`, for example after your own stale check or an \`exportImage\` with no \`attachImage\`. It does nothing while a cropper holds the photo.

zriz never closes an \`ImageBitmap\` you pass as the source. Close it yourself once the photo left its cropper, and not before.

## Handle a failed load

\`onError\` on \`Root\` gets a \`ZrizError\`. Its \`code\` names the problem, such as \`timeout\` or \`cross-origin\`. \`onLoad\` fires instead when the photo is ready, with its \`width\`, \`height\` and \`mime\`. [Errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists every code with its fix.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes playground as markdown 1`] = `
"# Playground

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Change the main props on a live cropper and copy the code that matches what you see.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/playground runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, the straighten dial with rotate and flip, and open, reset and download in the corners. The controls under it change its props.

## Change the props

### \`Root\`

- \`shape\`: \`rect\`, \`circle\`
- \`motion\`: \`auto\`, \`always\`, \`reduce\`
- \`dir\`: \`ltr\`, \`rtl\`
- \`maxZoom\`: from 1× to 15×
- \`recenterDelay\`: from 0 ms to 2000 ms
- \`disabled\`: on or off

### \`Viewport\`

- \`wheel\`: \`pan\`, \`zoom\`, \`none\`
- \`rotateGesture\`: on or off

## Tilt the photo

Perspective tilts the photo as if the camera leaned. No part sets it, so pass it in \`value\` or \`defaultValue\`, or call \`setValue()\`. [Value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md) has its range.

- \`vertical\`: from -30° to 30°
- \`horizontal\`: from -30° to 30°

## Copy the code

The code builds the cropper in the demo, with only the props you changed from their defaults:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={photo}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ height: 380 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
  <Cropper.Rotate aria-label="Rotate left" />
  <Cropper.DialValue />
  <Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten">
    <Cropper.DialTicks />
  </Cropper.Dial>
  <Cropper.Flip aria-label="Flip" />
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

The demo adds handles, a grid and reset, shows the angle to one decimal and uses the styles from the zriz site. On a phone its stage is 320 px tall, not the 380 in the code. It tilts the photo with \`setValue()\` and listens to \`onValueChange\` and \`onValueCommit\` for the value and the events below.

## Watch the value

The crop as you move it, the same object \`onValueChange\` and \`onValueCommit\` hand you:

\`\`\`json
{
  "aspect": "free",
  "center": {
    "x": 0.5,
    "y": 0.5
  },
  "mirrored": false,
  "perspective": {
    "horizontal": 0,
    "vertical": 0
  },
  "quarterTurns": 0,
  "rotation": 0,
  "size": {
    "height": 1,
    "width": 1
  }
}
\`\`\`

## Watch the events

Each row is a reason and a phase from \`onValueChange\`, or a commit from \`onValueCommit\`, as in [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md), newest first:

\`\`\`text
12:04:31  dial commit 4°
12:04:31  dial end
12:04:30  dial move
12:04:29  dial start
\`\`\`

## Export the crop

Download the crop as a PNG made by \`exportCrop\` with \`type: "image/png"\`. With \`shape\` set to \`"circle"\` above, the corners come out transparent.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes quick-start as markdown 1`] = `
"# Quick start

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Go from an empty project to a cropper that hands you the crop.

## Set it up with an agent

Paste this prompt into Claude Code, Cursor or any other coding agent. It reads this page and does the steps below for you:

\`\`\`text
Add zriz to this project. Read https://zriz.dev/docs/quick-start.md and follow it: install @zriz/react and @zriz/core, import its stylesheet and render a cropper with corner handles, the straighten dial and a file picker for the photo. Build it from the zriz parts, not a crop box of your own, and give every part that asks for one an aria-label. When it works, show me where onValueCommit hands back the crop.
\`\`\`

## Install

\`\`\`sh
pnpm add @zriz/react @zriz/core
\`\`\`

npm, Yarn and Bun work too. [FAQ](https://zriz.dev/docs/faq.md) lists what zriz needs.

## Render a cropper

Save this component in its own file and render \`<PhotoCropper />\` from a page. It loads a sample photo, and the stylesheet gives it the default look:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";

const photo = "https://picsum.photos/id/1015/1600/1200";

export const PhotoCropper = () => {
  return (
    <Cropper.Root src={photo}>
      <Cropper.Viewport
        style={{ maxWidth: 400, height: 300, padding: 24 }}
      >
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
          <Cropper.Handle position="top-left" />
          <Cropper.Handle position="top-right" />
          <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-right" />
          <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-left" />
        </Cropper.Area>
      </Cropper.Viewport>
      <Cropper.Dial
        aria-label="Straighten"
        style={{ maxWidth: 361 }}
      >
        <Cropper.DialTicks />
      </Cropper.Dial>
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`Viewport\` needs a size, and the image and the mask fill it. The 24 px padding leaves room for the handles, see [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md). The cropper now drags, pinches, resizes and straightens:

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/quick-start runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo with four corner handles, the straighten dial, and open, reset and download in the corners.

The demo above runs this component at the zriz site's size, with a public domain photo, open, reset and download buttons and the site's styles. Like the code, it shows no angle above the dial.

## Let the user pick a photo

Keep the chosen file in state and pass it to \`src\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useState } from "react";

export const PhotoCropper = () => {
  const [photo, setPhoto] = useState<File | null>(null);

  return (
    <>
      <input
        aria-label="Choose a photo"
        type="file"
        accept="image/*"
        onChange={(e) => setPhoto(e.target.files?.[0] ?? null)}
      />
      <Cropper.Root
        src={photo}
        onError={(error) => console.error(error.code)}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport
          style={{ maxWidth: 400, height: 300, padding: 24 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
            <Cropper.Handle position="top-left" />
            <Cropper.Handle position="top-right" />
            <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-right" />
            <Cropper.Handle position="bottom-left" />
          </Cropper.Area>
        </Cropper.Viewport>
        <Cropper.Dial
          aria-label="Straighten"
          style={{ maxWidth: 361 }}
        >
          <Cropper.DialTicks />
        </Cropper.Dial>
      </Cropper.Root>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`src\` takes a URL, a \`File\` or a \`Blob\`. While it is \`null\`, the viewport stays empty. When a photo cannot load, \`onError\` gets a \`ZrizError\`, and [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists its codes. A URL from another domain needs CORS headers, and a new photo keeps the current crop. [Loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) explains both.

## Get the crop

Add \`onValueCommit\` to \`Root\` in the picker above. It fires once when a gesture ends, a key press on the crop or the dial included, or on the frame after a write such as \`rotate90()\`. The first photo sends no commit until the user touches it, and a later photo sends one only when it has to refit the crop, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md).

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={photo}
  onError={(error) => console.error(error.code)}
  onValueCommit={(value, details) => {
    console.log(value, details.pixelCrop);
  }}
>
\`\`\`

\`value\` is the crop as fractions of the photo, ready to store and load back. [Value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md) lists every field. \`details.pixelCrop\` is the export plan for that crop in source pixels, or \`null\` before a photo loads. Its \`pixelRect\` is the crop rectangle, and it is \`null\` with a straighten angle or perspective. \`getPixelCrop()\` on the cropper from \`actionsRef\` reads it for an untouched photo, see [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md). [Exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md) turns the crop into a file in the browser or on your server.

[Playground](https://zriz.dev/docs/playground.md) changes the main props live, and [full editor](https://zriz.dev/docs/full-editor.md) puts every part in one component.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes root as markdown 1`] = `
"# Root

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`Root\` loads the photo, keeps the crop and holds every other part.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/root runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo on a bare cropper, and open, reset and download in the corners. Download locks the cropper while it saves.

## Anatomy

Everything goes inside \`Root\`. \`Image\`, \`Mask\` and \`Area\` go in \`Viewport\`. The controls can sit anywhere, in any wrapper:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={photo}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ height: 400 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area">
      <Cropper.Grid />
    </Cropper.Area>
  </Cropper.Viewport>
  <Cropper.Dial aria-label="Straighten">
    <Cropper.DialTicks />
  </Cropper.Dial>
  <div className="toolbar">
    <Cropper.Rotate>rotate</Cropper.Rotate>
    <Cropper.Flip>flip</Cropper.Flip>
    <Cropper.Reset>reset</Cropper.Reset>
  </div>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## Lock the cropper while you save

\`disabled\` ignores every gesture, key and button press, and every cropper method that moves the crop, such as \`rotate90()\` or \`setZoom()\`, except \`setValue()\`, \`setImage()\` and \`setOptions()\`. \`Root\` gets \`data-disabled\`, so CSS can grey it out. \`value\` and a new \`src\` still apply, and \`aspect\`, \`rotation\` and \`zoom\` set while disabled apply once it ends:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={photo} disabled={saving}>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ height: 400 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

Grey out the buttons while it is set:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="root"][data-disabled] button {
  opacity: 0.4;
  pointer-events: none;
}
\`\`\`

The demo above sets \`disabled\` while its download runs. It adds handles, a grid and the styles from the zriz site.

## Dim the controls during a gesture

\`Root\` carries data attributes for what the cropper is doing. This rule fades the toolbar during any gesture, a key press included, and for a short hold after it, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md):

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="root"][data-interacting] .toolbar {
  opacity: 0.4;
}
\`\`\`

## Put it in a drawer

\`Root\` carries \`data-vaul-no-drag\` and \`data-base-ui-swipe-ignore\`, so a swipe on the photo or a control moves the crop and leaves a Vaul or Base UI drawer where it is. Give the drawer a handle or free space outside \`Root\`. To let the drawer drag from inside, set the attribute to \`null\` on \`Root\`, such as \`data-vaul-no-drag={null}\`.

## API

### \`Root\`

Renders a \`<div>\`. Every other part goes inside it. Every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ snapshot, chrome }\`. \`snapshot\` holds the fields \`useCropperState\` lists on [hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md). \`chrome\` holds \`grid\`, \`active\`, \`transition\` and \`overscroll\`, the values behind \`data-grid\`, \`data-interacting\`, \`data-transition\` and \`data-overscroll\`.

- \`src\` \`string | Blob | null\`

  The photo: a URL or a \`Blob\` such as a \`File\`. \`null\` clears it, \`undefined\` leaves it alone
- \`crossOrigin\` \`"anonymous" | "use-credentials"\`

  The CORS mode when the photo is on another domain
- \`previewEdge\` \`number\`

  The longest side of the preview in pixels. The default follows the screen, see [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md). A grid of small \`Root\`s saves memory with a low one. A new \`previewEdge\`, \`crossOrigin\` or \`svg\` loads the photo again and fires \`onLoad\` again. The crop stays, so keep \`previewEdge\` steady
- \`svg\` \`boolean\` = \`true\`

  \`false\` refuses SVG files, which then fail with \`decode\`
- \`image\` \`Size | null\`

  The photo's pixel size up front, read once
- \`value\` \`CropValue\`

  Controls the whole value. \`onValueChange\` does not fire when you set it
- \`defaultValue\` \`Partial<CropValue>\`

  The starting value. Fields you leave out use the defaults
- \`aspect\` \`"free" | "original" | number\`

  Controls the aspect alone, a ratio like \`4 / 3\`
- \`rotation\` \`number\`

  Controls the straighten angle alone, up to 45° each way
- \`zoom\` \`number\`

  Controls the zoom alone. 1 is the largest crop
- \`shape\` \`"rect" | "circle"\` = \`"rect"\`

  The crop shape. A circle locks the aspect to 1, draws a round mask and exports a round PNG, and the stylesheet hides the corner handles, see [avatar](https://zriz.dev/docs/avatar.md). Back to \`"rect"\`, the crop takes the aspect it held before. A \`Root\` that started as a circle takes \`defaultValue\` or \`"free"\`, and reset follows
- \`minSize\` \`Size\` = \`{ width: 1, height: 1 }\`

  The smallest crop in source pixels, along the photo's own sides. A large one narrows the straighten range. [Exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md) says when a crop can come out smaller
- \`maxZoom\` \`number\` = \`15\`

  The deepest zoom. Every gesture, key and control stops there
- \`onValueChange\` \`(value, details) => void\`

  At most once per frame while the crop moves, with a reason and \`cancel()\`, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md)
- \`onValueCommit\` \`(value, details) => void\`

  Called once when a gesture or a write is done, with \`pixelCrop\`, the export plan, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md)
- \`onLoad\` \`(image) => void\`

  Called when the photo is decoded and ready to crop
- \`onError\` \`(error) => void\`

  Called with a typed error when the photo cannot load, see [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md)
- \`cropper\` \`Cropper\`

  Uses an instance from \`useCropper\`. \`image\`, \`defaultValue\`, \`plugins\` and \`focalLength\` then come from \`useCropper\`
- \`actionsRef\` \`Ref<Cropper>\`

  Gives you the cropper, for calls like \`rotate90()\` from a button
- \`plugins\` \`readonly CropperPlugin[]\`

  Runs each plugin's \`init\` once, when \`Root\` makes the cropper, see [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md)
- \`disabled\` \`boolean\` = \`false\`

  Ignores gestures, keys, buttons and the cropper methods that move the crop. \`value\` and \`setValue()\` still apply. Sets \`data-disabled\`
- \`motion\` \`"auto" | "always" | "reduce"\` = \`"auto"\`

  Follows the system motion setting, or forces full or short motion
- \`recenterDelay\` \`number\` = \`500\`

  Milliseconds after a gesture before the crop recentres
- \`dir\` \`"ltr" | "rtl"\`

  The reading direction for \`Dial\`, \`Zoom\` and \`AspectGroup\`. In RTL their left and right swap. \`Area\`'s arrow keys stay physical. With no value, each of these parts reads its computed \`direction\` at the press. With a value, \`Root\` also sets the HTML \`dir\` attribute
- \`locale\` \`string\`

  Formats the values screen readers hear
- \`focalLength\` \`number\` = \`26\`

  The lens as a 35 mm equivalent, used for perspective correction. At a strong tilt, zriz lengthens a lens too short to keep the photo in front of it. Read once, when \`Root\` makes the cropper. \`cropper.setOptions()\` changes it, from the next \`setValue()\`, \`reset()\` or \`setImage()\`

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-interacting\` | During a gesture and for a short hold after it, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) |
| \`data-panning\` | From a press on the photo until the pointer lets go, even if it never moves |
| \`data-pinching\` | While two fingers pinch or twist on a touch screen, or a trackpad pinches in Safari |
| \`data-resizing\` | From a press on a handle until the pointer lets go, even if it never moves |
| \`data-rotating\` | From a press on the dial until the pointer lets go, even if it never moves, and while the dial glides after |
| \`data-zooming\` | While the wheel pans or zooms, from a press on \`Zoom\` until the pointer lets go, or while a \`useZoom\` slider moves |
| \`data-settling\` | While any animation runs: a glide, a spring back, a recentre or a button's move |
| \`data-changed\` | While \`Reset\` has a change to undo |
| \`data-mirrored\` | While the photo is flipped |
| \`data-overscroll\` | While you pull past an edge or a zoom limit |
| \`data-disabled\` | While \`disabled\` is set |
| \`data-reduced-motion\` | While short motion is on |
| \`data-orientation\` | \`"landscape"\` above 1, \`"portrait"\` below 1, \`"square"\` at 1 and while the aspect is \`"free"\` or \`"original"\` |
| \`data-grid\` | \`"coarse"\`, \`"fine"\` or \`"none"\`, see [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md) |
| \`data-transition\` | \`"rotate"\`, \`"flip"\`, \`"morph"\` or \`"recenter"\` while that transition runs. \`"morph"\` covers reset, an aspect pick, an orientation swap and undo. A reset or undo across a quarter turn runs as \`"rotate"\`, which hides \`Area\` and shows only the crop turning with the photo |
| \`data-shape\` | \`"rect"\` or \`"circle"\` |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes saving-state as markdown 1`] = `
"# Saving state

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Keep the crop for each photo in local storage and bring it back on the next visit.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/saving-state runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, the straighten dial with rotate and flip, and open, reset and download in the corners. The crop is kept in local storage.

## Read the saved crop before the cropper mounts

Each photo gets its own key, built from an \`id\` you pass, such as the photo's id in your database. \`parse\` turns the stored string back into a crop, or returns \`null\` when there is none. [Value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md) shows what it does with broken input.

\`parse\` and \`serialize\` come from \`@zriz/core\`, which [quick start](https://zriz.dev/docs/quick-start.md) installs.

\`Root\` reads \`defaultValue\` once, when it mounts. The component renders nothing until it has read storage for this \`id\`. That empty render unmounts the old cropper, so each photo starts fresh.

Storage can refuse a read, such as in a sandboxed iframe. \`read\` treats that as no saved crop, so the cropper still opens.

\`\`\`tsx
const read = (key: string) => {
  try {
    return localStorage.getItem(key);
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
};

const key = \`zriz-crop:\${id}\`;

const [saved, setSaved] = useState<Saved>();

useEffect(() => {
  setSaved({ id, value: parse(read(key)) });
}, [id, key]);

if (saved?.id !== id) return null;
\`\`\`

## Start from it and save each commit

\`serialize\` writes a JSON string with a version field and stores the crop as fractions of the photo, so it fits any viewport size. To keep it in a database instead, send the same string with \`fetch\`.

\`Reset\` goes back to \`defaultValue\`, which is now the saved crop. To keep \`Reset\` on the whole photo, pass \`saved.value ?? undefined\` as \`value\` instead. It stays the same object, so it moves the crop only once, see [value](https://zriz.dev/docs/value.md).

The recipe skips \`coverage\` commits. That is zriz fitting the crop to a new photo, and nobody chose it, so it must not replace the saved crop. A full quota throws on write. Without the \`try\`, zriz rethrows that as an uncaught error, so the \`try\` keeps your error reporting quiet and the crop goes on working unsaved.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  src={src}
  defaultValue={saved.value ?? undefined}
  onValueCommit={(value, details) => {
    if (details.reason === "coverage") return;

    try {
      localStorage.setItem(key, serialize(value));
    } catch (error) {
      console.warn("the crop was not saved", error);
    }
  }}
>
  <Cropper.Viewport style={{ maxWidth: 640, height: 480 }}>
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask />
    <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

## The whole file

\`SavedCrop\` takes a photo URL and an id to store its crop under:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { parse, serialize } from "@zriz/core";
import type { CropValue } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

type Props = { id: string; src: string };
type Saved = { id: string; value: CropValue | null };

const read = (key: string) => {
  try {
    return localStorage.getItem(key);
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
};

export const SavedCrop = ({ id, src }: Props) => {
  const key = \`zriz-crop:\${id}\`;
  const [saved, setSaved] = useState<Saved>();

  useEffect(() => {
    setSaved({ id, value: parse(read(key)) });
  }, [id, key]);

  if (saved?.id !== id) return null;

  return (
    <Cropper.Root
      src={src}
      defaultValue={saved.value ?? undefined}
      onValueCommit={(value, details) => {
        if (details.reason === "coverage") return;

        try {
          localStorage.setItem(key, serialize(value));
        } catch (error) {
          console.warn("the crop was not saved", error);
        }
      }}
    >
      <Cropper.Viewport style={{ maxWidth: 640, height: 480 }}>
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
      </Cropper.Viewport>
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`

The demo above saves its crop in this browser under \`zriz-docs:saving-state:\` and an id for the photo. Change the crop and reload the page, and it comes back. The demo never renders on the server, so it reads storage as it mounts and skips the empty render.

It adds the dial, rotate, flip, handles, a grid and reset, shows the angle to one decimal and uses the styles from the zriz site.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes security as markdown 1`] = `
"# Security

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

What zriz handles about hostile files and private photos, and what stays your job.

## What zriz does on its own

zriz sends nothing anywhere. The photo stays in the browser until your code uploads something. The browser decodes it in a worker, and an export draws pixels onto a canvas and encodes them, so only the crop's pixels reach the file.

We ran files carrying each of these blocks through \`exportCrop\` in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, through the [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) route, and through plain sharp, then parsed the results:

| In the upload | \\\`exportCrop\\\` | Server crop route | sharp with \\\`keepMetadata()\\\` |
| - | - | - | - |
| GPS and EXIF | Gone | Gone | Kept |
| EXIF thumbnail of the whole photo | Gone | Gone | Kept |
| XMP and IPTC | Gone | Gone | Kept |
| Pixels outside the crop | Gone | Gone | Gone |
| A second image, video or other bytes after the image | Gone | Gone | Gone |
| Gain map | Gone | Gone | Gone, and \`keepGainMap()\` keeps a cropped one |

Safari writes a small EXIF block of its own into a JPEG or PNG, with only the crop's size and colour space.

## Treat the original as private

Cropping hides part of a photo, and the original still holds all of it. The file a person picks carries their location, the time, the camera, a thumbnail of the uncropped frame, and on many phones a short video. Someone who crops a face or a street sign out expects it to be gone.

What to do, best first:

| Choice | What you do |
| - | - |
| Upload the export | Send the blob from \`exportCrop\` and never the file. The original stays on the device |
| Cut on the server and drop the original | Use [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md), store the answer and let the upload go when the request ends. Don't write it to disk, to a queue or to logs |
| Keep a copy to crop again later | Store the copy below in a private bucket, never at a public URL and never beside the crop, and tell the person you keep the whole frame. Delete it when they delete the crop |

\`cleanCopy\` decodes the upload and encodes it again, which drops every block in the table. It keeps every pixel and fills clear ones white, as the route does, so it is still the whole photo:

\`\`\`ts
import sharp from "sharp";

export const cleanCopy = async (input: Buffer) =>
  await sharp(input, { limitInputPixels: 120_000_000 })
    .rotate()
    .flatten({ background: "#ffffff" })
    .jpeg({ quality: 92 })
    .toBuffer();
\`\`\`

On a pipeline opened from an upload, never call \`keepMetadata()\`, \`keepExif()\`, \`withMetadata()\`, \`withExif()\` or \`withExifMerge()\`. Each one kept the EXIF thumbnail in our test, so a crop can carry a small picture of the frame it cut away. To write your own tags, cut the photo to raw pixels first, as the route does, and add them to a new pipeline.

The crop value and \`details.pixelCrop\` tell where the crop sits and how large the original is. Keep them out of public URLs when that matters.

## Apple and phone formats

| File | What happens |
| - | - |
| HEIC and HEIF | Only Safari decodes them. Other browsers fail with \`unsupported-format\`, and prebuilt sharp has no HEVC decoder, so the route answers 415. A \`decode\` function, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md), sends the original to your server, so the rules above apply to it |
| Live photo | The still and the video are two files. zriz gets the file you pass and never sees the other. If your own picker uploads both, you upload the video too |
| Motion photo | The video sits after the image in one file. Exports and the route drop it, and the original keeps it |
| Gain map, MPF | A second image in the file for HDR screens, covering the whole frame. Exports and the route drop it, and the exported photo is SDR |
| P3 and other ICC profiles | The colour is converted on decode. An export is sRGB unless you ask for \`display-p3\`, and the only profile in the file is the browser's own |

## Decoder bugs

zriz has no image decoder of its own. In the browser the decoding is done by the browser and the operating system, and a page cannot patch them or see their version. Bugs there are real. CVE-2023-4863 in libwebp was exploited through Chrome before 116.0.5845.187, and CVE-2023-41064 in Apple's ImageIO was used against iPhones with no click. Opening a file in a cropper is the same exposure as showing it in an \`img\`. Tell people to keep their browser and system updated.

On your server, sharp is your decoder and you own its patches. Use 0.35.5 or newer, which bundles libvips 8.18.7. Versions before 0.35.0 are open to GHSA-f88m-g3jw-g9cj. Run \`npm audit\` in CI, and turn off the decoders you don't need with \`sharp.block()\`, as the route does. Don't point sharp at a libvips from your system, because that one brings its own loaders and its own patch level.

The [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) route answers 413 for a photo over 120 megapixels. \`MAX_PIXELS\` is the one constant to raise. A photo at that limit peaked at about 2 GB in our test, so raise it only with memory to match.

zriz reads the pixel count before it decodes and refuses a file over \`maxPixels\`. Lower it if your page only needs small photos, as in [loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md). It sets no limit on bytes, so check \`file.size\` before you pass a file.

## SVG

zriz draws an SVG through an \`img\` from a data URL, so the browser runs no script in it and fetches nothing it points at. We tried scripts, event attributes, \`foreignObject\`, external images, stylesheets, fonts and \`use\` in all three engines, with no script run and no request sent. The export is a bitmap, so nothing from the SVG source survives in it.

An SVG still draws on the main thread. A file under 2 kB froze the tab for 8 to 15 seconds in our test, and \`timeout\` cannot stop it. Pass \`svg={false}\` to \`Root\` when people upload photos and you have no use for SVG.

The danger is your own code using the source. A blob URL of a file runs with your origin when it opens as a document, so a script inside the SVG acts as your site. An \`img\` is safe. These are not:

| Never do this with an uploaded file |
| - |
| \`dangerouslySetInnerHTML\` or \`innerHTML\` with the text of the file |
| \`object\`, \`embed\` or \`iframe\` with its URL |
| A link or \`window.open\` to its \`blob:\` URL |

## Serve what people upload

The file name, the extension and the mime type come from the client, and a file can be a valid image and valid HTML at once. zriz reads the format from the bytes, and so does sharp, but your upload route decides how a file comes back to a browser.

For every file you serve back:

| Rule | Why |
| - | - |
| Encode it again and serve that, as \`cleanCopy\` does | Extra bytes in the file don't survive |
| Set \`content-type\` from what you encoded, never from the upload | A declared \`text/html\` or \`image/svg+xml\` would run |
| Send \`x-content-type-options: nosniff\` | The browser won't guess a type from the bytes |
| Send \`content-security-policy: default-src 'none'; sandbox\` | If a file does open as a document, it runs nothing |
| Serve from a separate domain, such as \`yourappusercontent.com\` | A file that slips through can't reach your cookies or your storage |
| Name the stored file with a random id | A name from the client can hold \`../\` or overwrite another file |

\`\`\`ts
export const serveUpload = (jpeg: Buffer) =>
  new Response(new Uint8Array(jpeg), {
    headers: {
      "content-type": "image/jpeg",
      "content-disposition": 'inline; filename="photo.jpg"',
      "content-security-policy": "default-src 'none'; sandbox",
      "x-content-type-options": "nosniff",
    },
  });
\`\`\`

## Set a content security policy

These sources let the cropper work with its worker. Merge them into your own policy. [Loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) says what each one enables, and what falls back when it is missing.

\`\`\`ts
export const cropperPolicy = [
  "img-src 'self' blob: data:",
  "worker-src blob:",
  "connect-src 'self'",
].join("; ");
\`\`\`

zriz injects no \`style\` tag and calls no \`innerHTML\` or \`eval\`, so \`script-src\` needs nothing for it, and \`style-src\` needs nothing for the parts' own styles. A \`style\` prop of yours on a server rendered part stays refused, and the recipes size \`Viewport\`, \`Dial\` and \`Zoom\` and paint \`ZoomThumb\` that way. Add \`style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'\` to allow it, or drop the \`style\` prop and give those parts a class from your stylesheet. The same goes for a \`style\` attribute in your own HTML, as in the [vanilla JS](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md) markup. A \`<style>\` element in your HTML is refused too, and \`style-src-attr\` does not cover it. Move its rules to your stylesheet, or list its hash in \`style-src\`. With trusted types, add \`zriz\` to \`trusted-types\`.

## URLs you pass as src

zriz fetches a string \`src\`, and the fetch sends cookies to your own origin. Never pass a URL a visitor typed, because it makes other visitors' browsers call any path on your site, and a host of the visitor's choice learns the IP address and page origin of everyone who opens it. Check the host against your own list first.

Pass \`crossOrigin="use-credentials"\` only for hosts you trust. To hide your page from the host, set \`referrerpolicy\` for the page with \`<meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer">\`.

Don't add a URL field to a server route. Fetching a URL from a client lets them reach your internal network. The [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) route takes a file for that reason.

A \`ZrizError\` message for an error status holds the URL as you passed it, cut at 80 characters, without the query or fragment. A CORS or network failure holds no URL. If a secret sits in your path, don't log the message.

## Protect the server route

The [server crop](https://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop.md) route refuses values it cannot read, oversize bodies and unused decoders. It keeps any other value inside the photo, so a forged one can only move the crop. It can't know who is calling, so connect \`canCrop\` to your session check, add a rate limit per user, and set a body limit at your proxy too.

A saved value is client input as well. \`parse\` returns a clean object or \`null\` for anything in \`localStorage\` or a database, and a cropper keeps a value inside the photo, so a tampered one can only move the crop.

## Report a problem

Report it privately at \\[GitHub]\\(https\\://github.com/pqoqubbw/zriz/security/advisories/new), not in a public issue.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes server-crop as markdown 1`] = `
"# Server crop

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Send the photo and its crop value to your server, and cut the full-size original there with sharp.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/server-crop runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, the straighten dial with rotate and flip, and open, reset and send in the corners. Send shows the value and the sharp plan.

## Install sharp

A browser export can come out smaller than the crop. [Loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) has the size limits, and [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) explains \`blank\`. sharp has no canvas and never retries smaller, so cut on the server when you need the full‑size crop.

The route runs on your server and needs \`sharp\`. Add it and \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md):

\`\`\`sh
pnpm add sharp @zriz/core
\`\`\`

Use sharp 0.35.5 or newer and keep it updated. It bundles libvips and every image decoder, so an old sharp means old decoders. [Security](https://zriz.dev/docs/security.md) lists the advisories.

## Send the file and the value

The demo above uploads nothing. Its send button works out the plan in the browser, the way the route does, and shows the value and the \`toSharp\` steps under the dial, with numbers rounded to four places.

\`getValue()\` returns the crop value, and \`serialize\` turns it into a small JSON string. Send that, not the export plan, so the server works out which pixels to keep on its own. The upload button waits for \`onLoad\`, because a value means nothing without its photo.

\`crop-limits.ts\` holds \`minSize\` and \`maxZoom\` for \`Root\` and for the route, so the server recomputes the plan the client saw. Change them there, and both ends stay the same.

A bad request comes back as text, and the alert shows it. A photo the browser cannot open shows the same alert.

Each pick bumps a counter that \`Root\` takes as its \`key\`, so every photo starts from the full frame. [Loading images](https://zriz.dev/docs/loading-images.md) explains why. Safari fires no \`change\` for the same file twice, so \`onClick\` clears the input first.

\`last\` holds the upload in flight. A new upload, a new pick or an unmount aborts it, so an answer for an old photo never shows under a new one. Starting an upload clears the old result and error, and the effect on \`result\` revokes each URL it leaves behind.

\`\`\`tsx
const unreadable =
  "this photo does not open. pick a jpeg, png, webp or avif.";

const [picks, setPicks] = useState(0);

const last = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);

useEffect(() => () => last.current?.abort(), []);

useEffect(
  () => () => {
    if (result) URL.revokeObjectURL(result);
  },
  [result]
);

const upload = async () => {
  const value = actions.current?.getValue();

  if (!file || !value) return;
  last.current?.abort();
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const { signal } = controller;

  last.current = controller;
  setError(null);
  setResult(null);
  const form = new FormData();

  form.append("file", file);
  form.append("value", serialize(value));

  try {
    const response = await fetch("/api/crop", {
      method: "POST",
      body: form,
      signal,
    });

    if (response.ok) {
      const blob = await response.blob();

      if (!signal.aborted) {
        setResult(URL.createObjectURL(blob));
      }
    } else {
      const message = await response.text();

      if (!signal.aborted) setError(message);
    }
  } catch {
    if (!signal.aborted) {
      setError("the upload did not reach the server.");
    }
  }
};

<input
  aria-label="Choose a photo"
  type="file"
  accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp,image/avif"
  onClick={(event) => {
    event.currentTarget.value = "";
  }}
  onChange={(event) => {
    last.current?.abort();
    setReady(false);
    setError(null);
    setResult(null);
    setPicks((count) => count + 1);
    setFile(event.target.files?.[0] ?? null);
  }}
/>

<Cropper.Root
  key={picks}
  actionsRef={actions}
  maxZoom={MAX_ZOOM}
  minSize={MIN_SIZE}
  src={file}
  svg={false}
  onLoad={() => setReady(true)}
  onError={() => setError(unreadable)}
>

<button type="button" disabled={!ready} onClick={upload}>
  upload
</button>
\`\`\`

## Cut the original with sharp

\`rotate()\` with no angle turns the upload upright from its EXIF tag. The route reads the value with \`parse\`, builds a cropper at the size of that upright image and asks it for the plan. A value out of range lands inside the photo, the way it does in the browser. \`toSharp\` then turns the plan into steps for sharp.

A crop with no straighten comes back as \`"extract"\`: a rectangle, a flop, a turn in steps of 90° and a resize to the size of the plan. A straightened crop comes back as \`"affine"\`: extract the \`source\` window, the part of the photo the crop can touch, pad at least 2 px on each side by copying the edge, turn with a bicubic affine, then extract the crop.

In one pipeline sharp runs \`extend()\` after \`affine()\`, and \`affine()\` after \`extract()\` and \`rotate()\`, so the route ends each stage as a raw buffer and opens a new pipeline for the next one. Each new pipeline passes \`limitInputPixels: false\`, since the upload already passed the check and a turned crop can grow past it. The first one holds the limit.

\`toSharp\` does not apply perspective, so the route answers 422 for a value with \`perspective\`. Export those in the browser instead.

\`\`\`ts
type Raw = { data: Buffer; info: OutputInfo };
const open = (image: Raw) =>
  sharp(image.data, {
    raw: image.info,
    limitInputPixels: false,
  });
const raw = (pipeline: Sharp) =>
  pipeline
    .timeout({ seconds: MAX_SECONDS })
    .raw()
    .toBuffer({ resolveWithObject: true });

const cut = async (input: Buffer, value: string) => {
  const saved = parse(value);

  if (!saved) throw new TypeError("not a crop value");
  const upright = await raw(
    sharp(input, { limitInputPixels: MAX_PIXELS }).rotate()
  );
  const cropper = createCropper({
    image: upright.info,
    maxZoom: MAX_ZOOM,
    minSize: MIN_SIZE,
  });

  cropper.setValue(saved);
  const plan = cropper.getPixelCrop();

  if (!plan || plan.homography)
    throw new RangeError("no flat plan");
  const steps = toSharp(plan, upright.info);

  if (steps.kind === "extract") {
    const turned = await raw(
      open(upright)
        .extract(steps.extract)
        .flop(steps.flop)
        .rotate(steps.rotate)
    );

    return open(turned).resize({
      ...steps.resize,
      fit: "fill",
    });
  }

  const [[a, b], [c, d]] = steps.affine;
  const padded = await raw(
    open(upright).extract(steps.source).extend(steps.extend)
  );
  const turned = await raw(
    open(padded).affine([a, b, c, d], steps.options)
  );

  return open(turned).extract(steps.extract);
};
\`\`\`

The upload is cut as pixels, and each stage drops everything else, so EXIF, GPS, XMP, thumbnails, gain maps and bytes after the image never reach the answer. Don't add \`keepMetadata()\` or \`keepExif()\`. [Security](https://zriz.dev/docs/security.md) explains why.

## Answer the request

The route is a Next.js route handler saved as \`app/api/crop/route.ts\`. A JPEG at quality 92 with clear pixels filled white comes back when all goes well. Otherwise:

| Status | When |
| - | - |
| \`400\` | The form lacks the file or the value, or sharp cannot open the file |
| \`401\` | \`canCrop\` says no |
| \`413\` | The body is over 50 MB or declares no \`content-length\`, or the photo is over \`MAX_PIXELS\` |
| \`415\` | A HEIC or other HEIF that is not AVIF, because prebuilt sharp has no HEVC decoder |
| \`422\` | The crop cannot be cut, or the value is over 4096 characters |
| \`503\` | \`MAX_RUNNING\` crops are already running in this process |

Chromium and Firefox ignore the orientation tag of a WebP and Safari honours it, so the value can describe a different frame from the one sharp turns upright. The route answers 422 for a WebP with an orientation above 1 instead of cutting the wrong pixels.

\`canCrop\` in \`can-crop.ts\` answers \`false\` until you connect your session check, so a copied route refuses everyone. Replace its body before you ship.

The value and the file come from the browser, so treat both as hostile. The top of the route holds the limits:

\`\`\`ts
const MAX_BODY = 50 * 1024 * 1024;
const MAX_VALUE = 4096;
const MAX_PIXELS = 120_000_000;
const MAX_RUNNING = 2;
const MAX_SECONDS = 30;
const HEADERS = {
  "cache-control": "private, no-store",
  "x-content-type-options": "nosniff",
};

sharp.block({ operation: ["VipsForeignLoad"] });

sharp.unblock({
  operation: [
    "VipsForeignLoadJpegBuffer",
    "VipsForeignLoadPngBuffer",
    "VipsForeignLoadWebpBuffer",
    "VipsForeignLoadHeifBuffer",
  ],
});
\`\`\`

A small PNG can ask for a huge image, and the bytes on the wire say nothing about the memory it takes. A photo at \`MAX_PIXELS\` peaked at about 2 GB in our test, for a 460 kB upload at a 45° tilt, so give the server that much per running crop. \`MAX_PIXELS\` is the one constant to raise, and only with memory to match. 120 million covers a 108 megapixel phone photo, and a 200 megapixel one gets 413. \`MAX_RUNNING\` counts one process, so add a rate limit per user on top.

\`sharp.block()\` turns off every decoder but JPEG, PNG, WebP and AVIF for the whole process, because the route never needs SVG, TIFF, GIF or the others. Move it if another route in the same process does.

\`\`\`ts
const readForm = async (request: Request) => {
  let seen = 0;
  const capped = request.body?.pipeThrough(
    new TransformStream<Uint8Array, Uint8Array>({
      transform(chunk, stream) {
        seen += chunk.length;
        if (seen > MAX_BODY) stream.error(new RangeError());
        else stream.enqueue(chunk);
      },
    })
  );
  const form = await new Response(capped, {
    headers: request.headers,
  })
    .formData()
    .catch(() => null);

  return { form, over: seen > MAX_BODY };
};

const answer = async (request: Request) => {
  const { form, over } = await readForm(request);

  if (over) return refuse("this upload is too large.", 413);
  const file = form?.get("file");
  const value = form?.get("value");

  if (
    !(file instanceof File) ||
    file.size === 0 ||
    typeof value !== "string"
  ) {
    return refuse("send a file and a crop value.", 400);
  }

  if (value.length > MAX_VALUE) {
    return refuse("this crop does not fit this photo.", 422);
  }

  const input = Buffer.from(await file.arrayBuffer());
  const meta = await sharp(input)
    .metadata()
    .catch(() => null);

  if (!meta) return refuse("sharp cannot open this file.", 400);

  if (meta.format === "heif" && meta.compression !== "av1") {
    return refuse("send a jpeg, png, webp or avif.", 415);
  }

  if (meta.width * meta.height > MAX_PIXELS) {
    return refuse("this photo has too many pixels.", 413);
  }

  if (meta.format === "webp" && (meta.orientation ?? 1) > 1) {
    return refuse(
      "save this webp without an orientation tag.",
      422
    );
  }

  try {
    const crop = await cut(input, value);
    const jpeg = await crop
      .timeout({ seconds: MAX_SECONDS })
      .flatten({ background: "#ffffff" })
      .jpeg({ quality: 92 })
      .toBuffer();

    return new Response(new Uint8Array(jpeg), {
      headers: { ...HEADERS, "content-type": "image/jpeg" },
    });
  } catch {
    return refuse("this crop does not fit this photo.", 422);
  }
};
\`\`\`

\`\`\`ts
export const POST = async (request: Request) => {
  if (!(await canCrop(request))) {
    return refuse("sign in to crop a photo.", 401);
  }

  const length = Number(request.headers.get("content-length"));

  if (!(length > 0) || length > MAX_BODY) {
    return refuse("this upload is too large.", 413);
  }

  if (running >= MAX_RUNNING) {
    return refuse("the server is busy. try again.", 503);
  }

  running += 1;

  try {
    return await answer(request);
  } finally {
    running -= 1;
  }
};
\`\`\`

## All four files

Save \`crop-limits.ts\`, \`can-crop.ts\` and \`crop-upload.tsx\` next to the \`app\` folder. That is where the imports look for them.

\`crop-limits.ts\` is shared by both:

\`\`\`ts
export const MIN_SIZE = { width: 1, height: 1 };
export const MAX_ZOOM = 15;
\`\`\`

\`can-crop.ts\` runs on the server. Connect your session check here:

\`\`\`ts
export const canCrop = async (_request: Request) => false;
\`\`\`

\`crop-upload.tsx\` runs in the browser:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { serialize } from "@zriz/core";
import type { Cropper as CropperApi } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import { MAX_ZOOM, MIN_SIZE } from "./crop-limits";

const unreadable =
  "this photo does not open. pick a jpeg, png, webp or avif.";

export const CropUpload = () => {
  const [file, setFile] = useState<File | null>(null);
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [result, setResult] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [picks, setPicks] = useState(0);
  const actions = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const last = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => () => last.current?.abort(), []);

  useEffect(
    () => () => {
      if (result) URL.revokeObjectURL(result);
    },
    [result]
  );

  const upload = async () => {
    const value = actions.current?.getValue();

    if (!file || !value) return;
    last.current?.abort();
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const { signal } = controller;

    last.current = controller;
    setError(null);
    setResult(null);
    const form = new FormData();

    form.append("file", file);
    form.append("value", serialize(value));

    try {
      const response = await fetch("/api/crop", {
        method: "POST",
        body: form,
        signal,
      });

      if (response.ok) {
        const blob = await response.blob();

        if (!signal.aborted) {
          setResult(URL.createObjectURL(blob));
        }
      } else {
        const message = await response.text();

        if (!signal.aborted) setError(message);
      }
    } catch {
      if (!signal.aborted) {
        setError("the upload did not reach the server.");
      }
    }
  };

  return (
    <>
      <input
        aria-label="Choose a photo"
        type="file"
        accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp,image/avif"
        onClick={(event) => {
          event.currentTarget.value = "";
        }}
        onChange={(event) => {
          last.current?.abort();
          setReady(false);
          setError(null);
          setResult(null);
          setPicks((count) => count + 1);
          setFile(event.target.files?.[0] ?? null);
        }}
      />
      <Cropper.Root
        key={picks}
        actionsRef={actions}
        maxZoom={MAX_ZOOM}
        minSize={MIN_SIZE}
        src={file}
        svg={false}
        onLoad={() => setReady(true)}
        onError={() => setError(unreadable)}
      >
        <Cropper.Viewport
          style={{ maxWidth: 640, height: 480 }}
        >
          <Cropper.Image />
          <Cropper.Mask />
          <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
        </Cropper.Viewport>
        <Cropper.Dial
          aria-label="Straighten"
          style={{ maxWidth: 361 }}
        >
          <Cropper.DialTicks />
        </Cropper.Dial>
      </Cropper.Root>
      <button type="button" disabled={!ready} onClick={upload}>
        upload
      </button>
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
      {result && (
        <img src={result} alt="Crop from the server" />
      )}
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`app/api/crop/route.ts\` runs on the server:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper, parse, toSharp } from "@zriz/core";
import sharp from "sharp";
import type { OutputInfo, Sharp } from "sharp";
import { canCrop } from "../../../can-crop";
import { MAX_ZOOM, MIN_SIZE } from "../../../crop-limits";

const MAX_BODY = 50 * 1024 * 1024;
const MAX_VALUE = 4096;
const MAX_PIXELS = 120_000_000;
const MAX_RUNNING = 2;
const MAX_SECONDS = 30;
const HEADERS = {
  "cache-control": "private, no-store",
  "x-content-type-options": "nosniff",
};

sharp.block({ operation: ["VipsForeignLoad"] });

sharp.unblock({
  operation: [
    "VipsForeignLoadJpegBuffer",
    "VipsForeignLoadPngBuffer",
    "VipsForeignLoadWebpBuffer",
    "VipsForeignLoadHeifBuffer",
  ],
});

let running = 0;

const refuse = (text: string, status: number) =>
  new Response(text, { headers: HEADERS, status });

type Raw = { data: Buffer; info: OutputInfo };
const open = (image: Raw) =>
  sharp(image.data, {
    raw: image.info,
    limitInputPixels: false,
  });
const raw = (pipeline: Sharp) =>
  pipeline
    .timeout({ seconds: MAX_SECONDS })
    .raw()
    .toBuffer({ resolveWithObject: true });

const cut = async (input: Buffer, value: string) => {
  const saved = parse(value);

  if (!saved) throw new TypeError("not a crop value");
  const upright = await raw(
    sharp(input, { limitInputPixels: MAX_PIXELS }).rotate()
  );
  const cropper = createCropper({
    image: upright.info,
    maxZoom: MAX_ZOOM,
    minSize: MIN_SIZE,
  });

  cropper.setValue(saved);
  const plan = cropper.getPixelCrop();

  if (!plan || plan.homography)
    throw new RangeError("no flat plan");
  const steps = toSharp(plan, upright.info);

  if (steps.kind === "extract") {
    const turned = await raw(
      open(upright)
        .extract(steps.extract)
        .flop(steps.flop)
        .rotate(steps.rotate)
    );

    return open(turned).resize({
      ...steps.resize,
      fit: "fill",
    });
  }

  const [[a, b], [c, d]] = steps.affine;
  const padded = await raw(
    open(upright).extract(steps.source).extend(steps.extend)
  );
  const turned = await raw(
    open(padded).affine([a, b, c, d], steps.options)
  );

  return open(turned).extract(steps.extract);
};

const readForm = async (request: Request) => {
  let seen = 0;
  const capped = request.body?.pipeThrough(
    new TransformStream<Uint8Array, Uint8Array>({
      transform(chunk, stream) {
        seen += chunk.length;
        if (seen > MAX_BODY) stream.error(new RangeError());
        else stream.enqueue(chunk);
      },
    })
  );
  const form = await new Response(capped, {
    headers: request.headers,
  })
    .formData()
    .catch(() => null);

  return { form, over: seen > MAX_BODY };
};

const answer = async (request: Request) => {
  const { form, over } = await readForm(request);

  if (over) return refuse("this upload is too large.", 413);
  const file = form?.get("file");
  const value = form?.get("value");

  if (
    !(file instanceof File) ||
    file.size === 0 ||
    typeof value !== "string"
  ) {
    return refuse("send a file and a crop value.", 400);
  }

  if (value.length > MAX_VALUE) {
    return refuse("this crop does not fit this photo.", 422);
  }

  const input = Buffer.from(await file.arrayBuffer());
  const meta = await sharp(input)
    .metadata()
    .catch(() => null);

  if (!meta) return refuse("sharp cannot open this file.", 400);

  if (meta.format === "heif" && meta.compression !== "av1") {
    return refuse("send a jpeg, png, webp or avif.", 415);
  }

  if (meta.width * meta.height > MAX_PIXELS) {
    return refuse("this photo has too many pixels.", 413);
  }

  if (meta.format === "webp" && (meta.orientation ?? 1) > 1) {
    return refuse(
      "save this webp without an orientation tag.",
      422
    );
  }

  try {
    const crop = await cut(input, value);
    const jpeg = await crop
      .timeout({ seconds: MAX_SECONDS })
      .flatten({ background: "#ffffff" })
      .jpeg({ quality: 92 })
      .toBuffer();

    return new Response(new Uint8Array(jpeg), {
      headers: { ...HEADERS, "content-type": "image/jpeg" },
    });
  } catch {
    return refuse("this crop does not fit this photo.", 422);
  }
};

export const POST = async (request: Request) => {
  if (!(await canCrop(request))) {
    return refuse("sign in to crop a photo.", 401);
  }

  const length = Number(request.headers.get("content-length"));

  if (!(length > 0) || length > MAX_BODY) {
    return refuse("this upload is too large.", 413);
  }

  if (running >= MAX_RUNNING) {
    return refuse("the server is busy. try again.", 503);
  }

  running += 1;

  try {
    return await answer(request);
  } finally {
    running -= 1;
  }
};
\`\`\`

The demo above keeps the limits and parts of \`crop-upload.tsx\`. It adds rotate, flip, handles, a grid and reset, shows the angle to one decimal and uses the styles from the zriz site.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes styling as markdown 1`] = `
"# Styling

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

The parts carry no colour. Paint them with the optional stylesheet, your own CSS or Tailwind.

## Add the stylesheet

Import the stylesheet once. [Bundle size](https://zriz.dev/docs/bundle-size.md) lists its weight:

\`\`\`ts
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
\`\`\`

| Part | What the stylesheet sets |
| - | - |
| \`Viewport\` | \`overflow: clip\` and a grab cursor |
| \`Mask\` | The scrim colour. It fades to 60% during a gesture |
| \`Area\` | A 1 px outline inside the crop, a two-tone ring on keyboard focus, and \`opacity: 0\` on it and its handles while \`Root\` has \`data-transition="rotate"\` |
| \`Handle\` | A corner mark of two short lines or an edge bar, and a resize cursor. Corners hide with \`shape="circle"\` |
| \`Grid\` | White lines that fade in during a gesture |
| \`Dial\` | 40 px height, sideways clip, a grab cursor, centre line, tick colours, the zero dot |

It paints nothing on \`Zoom\`, the buttons or the aspect items. The demos on this site style those themselves. [Viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md) shows how to size the box.

Every rule sits in \`:where()\` inside \`@layer components.zriz\`. A class with no layer wins in any import order, and so does a class in your \`components\` or \`utilities\` layer, Tailwind included. A class in any other layer wins only when that layer is declared after the stylesheet. \`@zriz/core/styles.css\` is the same file for [vanilla JS](https://zriz.dev/docs/vanilla-js.md).

A page-wide \`transition\` cannot make the crop trail your finger. zriz sets \`transition-property\` inline to \`none\` on \`Area\`, \`DialTicks\` and \`ZoomThumb\`, and to \`opacity\` on \`Mask\`, so the mask keeps its fade. A transition of your own on those parts needs \`!important\`.

The first line of the file names \`properties\`, \`theme\`, \`base\`, \`components\` and \`utilities\`, so a stylesheet loaded first sets the order of those layers on your page. To keep your own order, and to let every layer of yours win, import it into a layer of its own:

\`\`\`css
@layer vendor, reset, base, components, utilities;
@import url("@zriz/react/styles.css") layer(vendor);
\`\`\`

## Style a part by its state

\`className\` takes a string, or a function that gets the part's state:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Grid
  className={(state) =>
    state.grid === "fine" ? "opacity-50" : undefined
  }
/>
\`\`\`

Return \`undefined\` from the function for no class. To swap the element itself, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md).

## Style with data attributes

Every part's outer element carries \`data-zriz-item\` with its part name in kebab case, such as \`aspect-item\` or \`dial-ticks\`. The ticks and grid lines inside carry none, so select them through their part, as [dial](https://zriz.dev/docs/dial.md) shows. \`Root\` also marks the gesture in progress with \`data-rotating\`, \`data-panning\`, \`data-pinching\`, \`data-resizing\` or \`data-zooming\`. This rule hides the handles from a press on the dial until the pointer lets go, and while the dial glides:

\`\`\`css
[data-rotating] [data-zriz-item="handle"] {
  opacity: 0;
}
\`\`\`

Each component page lists the attributes that part sets.

## Change the colours

Set the colour variables on \`Root\`, on one part or on any ancestor. This one makes the scrim white in light mode and darker in dark mode, and the accent blue:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="root"] {
  --zriz-scrim: light-dark(#ffffffd9, #000000d9);
  --zriz-accent: #0a84ff;
}
\`\`\`

| Variable | Light, dark | What it sets |
| - | - | - |
| \`--zriz-scrim\` | \`#f7f7f7eb\`, \`#000000c7\` | The mask outside the crop |
| \`--zriz-line\` | \`#000\`, \`#fff\` | Outline, handle marks, dial centre line, major ticks, zero dot |
| \`--zriz-grid\` | \`#fff9\` in both | Grid lines |
| \`--zriz-tick\` | \`#949494\`, \`#666\` | Minor ticks on the dial |
| \`--zriz-accent\` | \`#fdca00\` in both | Dial centre line while you drag the dial |

Write a value with \`light-dark()\` to keep both schemes, or a single colour to use it in both.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/styling runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, the straighten dial with its value, a track of default, ink and soft colours, and open, reset and download in the corners.

The demo above switches between the stylesheet's colours and two light looks of its own. Each look sets all five variables on \`Root\`. Drag the dial to see the accent.

## Follow dark mode

The parts follow the page's \`color-scheme\`. The light values above apply in light mode and the dark values in dark mode.

With this rule on the page, the parts follow the system setting:

\`\`\`css
:root {
  color-scheme: light dark;
}
\`\`\`

To pin one part, put \`data-theme="dark"\` or the class \`dark\` on the part itself. It wins over a themed wrapper. \`light\` works on the part itself, but its children stay dark inside a dark wrapper:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Dial data-theme="dark" aria-label="Straighten" />
\`\`\`

In forced colours mode, the marks the stylesheet paints switch to \`CanvasText\`, and the dial's centre line to \`Highlight\` during a drag. The browser turns the scrim into a veil of \`Canvas\`, so a dark \`--zriz-scrim\` shows in the palette's own colour. The other \`--zriz-*\` variables keep your values, on \`Root\` or on one part, so CSS of your own that reads one needs a \`forced-colors\` rule with a system colour.

## Style with Tailwind

Without the stylesheet, utilities paint everything, the viewport's clip included. Tailwind's \`data-*\` variants read the state attributes:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root src={file}>
  <Cropper.Viewport className="h-80 overflow-clip">
    <Cropper.Image />
    <Cropper.Mask className="bg-black/60" />
    <Cropper.Area
      aria-label="Crop area"
      className="ring-1 ring-white data-interacting:ring-2 forced-colors:border"
    />
  </Cropper.Viewport>
</Cropper.Root>
\`\`\`

The mask turns 60% black, and the crop gets a white ring that thickens during a gesture. Forced colours mode removes the ring, because it is a box shadow, so \`forced-colors:border\` draws the frame there.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes undo as markdown 1`] = `
"# Undo

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

An undo button for the crop, built from what each commit hands you.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/undo runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, the straighten dial with rotate and undo, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Remember the crop before each gesture

\`details.previous\` holds the crop from before the gesture. One gesture is one commit, however many frames it took. The stack keeps the last 50.

\`remember\` skips commits with two reasons. \`history\` is the undo itself, and \`coverage\` is zriz fitting the crop to new limits.

\`\`\`tsx
const skipped = new Set(["history", "coverage"]);

const [history, setHistory] = useState<CropValue[]>([]);

const remember = (_: CropValue, details: CommitDetails) => {
  if (skipped.has(details.reason)) return;

  setHistory((stack) =>
    [...stack, details.previous].slice(-50)
  );
};
\`\`\`

## Step back

\`setValue()\` commits like any other change, with the reason you give it. A press during a gesture does nothing, because the gesture would overwrite the step and the stack would lose it. The button reads as off while the stack is empty, with \`aria-disabled\`, so it keeps focus when the last step goes.

For redo, keep a second stack. \`undo\` pushes \`actions.current.getValue()\` onto it before it steps back. A \`redo\` function pops it, pushes the current crop onto the undo stack and calls \`setValue()\` with \`history\` too. \`remember\` clears the redo stack after the reason check, so a new gesture drops it.

\`\`\`tsx
const undo = () => {
  const previous = history.at(-1);

  if (!previous || actions.current?.getState().interacting) {
    return;
  }

  setHistory(history.slice(0, -1));
  actions.current?.setValue(previous, { reason: "history" });
};

<button
  type="button"
  className="undo"
  aria-disabled={history.length === 0}
  onClick={undo}
>
  undo
</button>
\`\`\`

The browser dims only \`disabled\`, so paint the off state yourself. Save this as \`undo.css\` next to the editor, which imports it:

\`\`\`css
.undo[aria-disabled="true"] {
  opacity: 0.4;
}
\`\`\`

## Wire it to the cropper

\`onValueCommit\` hands every commit to \`remember\`. Crops from the last photo make no sense on the next one, so \`UndoEditor\` keys \`Editor\` by \`src\`. Each photo gets a fresh cropper at the full frame and an empty stack, and a late commit from the old cropper has no stack to reach. The old photo leaves the screen at once.

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Root
  actionsRef={actions}
  src={src}
  onValueCommit={remember}
>

export const UndoEditor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => (
  <Editor key={src} src={src} />
);
\`\`\`

## The whole file

\`Editor\` adds the button under the dial:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import type {
  CommitDetails,
  Cropper as CropperApi,
  CropValue,
} from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import "@zriz/react/styles.css";
import { useRef, useState } from "react";
import "./undo.css";

const skipped = new Set(["history", "coverage"]);

const Editor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const actions = useRef<CropperApi>(null);
  const [history, setHistory] = useState<CropValue[]>([]);

  const remember = (_: CropValue, details: CommitDetails) => {
    if (skipped.has(details.reason)) return;

    setHistory((stack) =>
      [...stack, details.previous].slice(-50)
    );
  };

  const undo = () => {
    const previous = history.at(-1);

    if (!previous || actions.current?.getState().interacting) {
      return;
    }

    setHistory(history.slice(0, -1));
    actions.current?.setValue(previous, { reason: "history" });
  };

  return (
    <Cropper.Root
      actionsRef={actions}
      src={src}
      onValueCommit={remember}
    >
      <Cropper.Viewport style={{ maxWidth: 640, height: 480 }}>
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
      </Cropper.Viewport>
      <Cropper.Dial
        aria-label="Straighten"
        style={{ maxWidth: 361 }}
      >
        <Cropper.DialTicks />
      </Cropper.Dial>
      <button
        type="button"
        className="undo"
        aria-disabled={history.length === 0}
        onClick={undo}
      >
        undo
      </button>
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};

export const UndoEditor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => (
  <Editor key={src} src={src} />
);
\`\`\`

The demo above runs this file with a rotate button, so a turn is one more step to undo. It adds handles, a grid and reset, shows the angle to one decimal and uses the styles from the zriz site.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes value as markdown 1`] = `
"# Value

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

A plain object that describes the crop. Read it to save a crop, and set it to restore or control one.

A square crop of a 4032 × 3024 photo, half its height, right of the middle and a little above it, straightened 3°:

\`\`\`ts
import type { CropValue } from "@zriz/react";

const value: CropValue = {
  center: { x: 0.6, y: 0.45 },
  size: { width: 0.375, height: 0.5 },
  rotation: 3,
  quarterTurns: 0,
  mirrored: false,
  perspective: { vertical: 0, horizontal: 0 },
  aspect: 1,
};
\`\`\`

Outside React, import the type from \`@zriz/core\`, installed as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md).

\`onValueCommit\`, \`getValue()\` and \`useCropperValue\` all hand you this object. \`Root\` and \`createCropper\` take the whole object as \`value\`, or part of it as \`defaultValue\`.

## Fields

- \`center\` \`Point\` = \`{ x: 0.5, y: 0.5 }\`

  Marks the middle of the crop. 0.5, 0.5 is the middle of the photo
- \`size\` \`Size\` = \`{ width: 1, height: 1 }\`

  Sets the crop's width and height as fractions of the photo's sides
- \`rotation\` \`number\` = \`0\`

  Straightens the photo, in degrees from −45 to 45. A \`minSize\` with a diagonal longer than a side of the photo lowers the limit, see \`getRotationLimit()\` on [cropper](https://zriz.dev/docs/cropper.md)
- \`quarterTurns\` \`0 | 1 | 2 | 3\` = \`0\`

  Counts clockwise 90° turns. One counter-clockwise turn reads 3
- \`mirrored\` \`boolean\` = \`false\`

  Swaps left and right. Each flip toggles it and negates \`rotation\`. A left to right flip also turns a \`quarterTurns\` of 1 into 3, and a top to bottom flip turns 0 into 2
- \`perspective\` \`object\` = \`{ vertical: 0, horizontal: 0 }\`

  Tilts the photo by \`vertical\` and \`horizontal\` degrees. No part or gesture sets it, only \`setValue()\`, \`setCropState()\`, \`value\` and \`defaultValue\`. Keep each within −30 to 30. \`parse\` clamps to that range, \`setValue()\` does not
- \`aspect\` \`Aspect\` = \`"free"\`

  Locks the crop's shape to a ratio such as \`16 / 9\`, to \`"original"\` for the photo's own shape, or to nothing with \`"free"\`

\`center\` and \`size\` are fractions of the photo after its quarter turns, never pixels. The same value fits a 400 px preview and the 4032 px original.

zriz measures each side on its own. The largest square crop of a 4032 × 3024 photo reads \`size: { width: 0.75, height: 1 }\`.

There is no zoom field. Zoom follows from \`size\`, and \`getZoom()\` returns it: 1 is the largest crop that fits.

## See how zriz fixes a bad value

zriz keeps every value you set valid. It clamps the angle, reshapes a \`size\` that misses its \`aspect\` at the same area, grows a crop below \`minSize\` or past \`maxZoom\`, and shrinks or moves a crop that would show an empty corner. Ask for a 90° straighten and you get the limit:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper({
  image: { width: 4032, height: 3024 },
});

cropper.setValue({ rotation: 90 });
cropper.getValue().rotation; // 45
\`\`\`

A field that is not a usable number keeps the value it had, and a write with a usable field that still cannot make a crop, such as \`setRotation(NaN)\`, is ignored. An \`aspect\` that is not a number from 0.000001 to 1000000 counts as \`"free"\`. An option that is not a positive number falls back to its default, but any \`maxZoom\` below 1, zero and negatives included, counts as 1.

A value from zriz is frozen, so one reader cannot change it for the next. Copy it to change a field: \`setValue({ ...value, rotation: 2 })\`.

Before a photo loads, zriz stores a value as you set it and fixes it when the photo arrives.

## Control the angle from React state

Pass \`rotation\` and update it in \`onValueChange\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";
import { useState } from "react";

export const Editor = ({ src }: { src: string }) => {
  const [rotation, setRotation] = useState(0);

  return (
    <Cropper.Root
      src={src}
      rotation={rotation}
      onValueChange={(value) => setRotation(value.rotation)}
    >
      <Cropper.Viewport style={{ maxWidth: 480, height: 360 }}>
        <Cropper.Image />
        <Cropper.Mask />
        <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
      </Cropper.Viewport>
      <Cropper.Dial
        aria-label="Straighten"
        style={{ maxWidth: 361 }}
      >
        <Cropper.DialTicks />
      </Cropper.Dial>
    </Cropper.Root>
  );
};
\`\`\`

\`rotation\`, \`zoom\` and \`aspect\` each control one part of the crop. A new prop moves the crop and commits, so \`onValueCommit\` runs with the whole new value. \`onValueChange\` stays quiet when each controlled prop lands as passed, even when the crop size moves with it, as it does for a new \`aspect\`. It runs when zriz applied something else: a rotation of 60 reports 45. To mirror the whole value, write it from both callbacks. A value zriz reported comes back as an answer and does not move the crop, for 2 s after the last report, so a slow store cannot rewind it. If no other answer follows and the crop has not moved since, that value applies when the 2 s end, or 500 ms after it arrived if that is later. A preset equal to a recent report lands late this way, and a value never reported lands at once. Mirror \`rotation\`, \`zoom\` or \`aspect\` from \`onValueChange\`, not \`onValueCommit\`: a store slower than 2 s that mirrors commits still flips the crop. Zoom runs from 1 up to \`maxZoom\`. A prop applied as the photo arrives is silent.

\`value\` sets the whole object without calling either callback. Each new object moves the crop, unless zriz reported that same object in the last 2 s, so keep it stable or update it from \`onValueChange\`. \`Reset\` still goes to \`defaultValue\`, not to the last \`value\`.

During a gesture and until it commits, zriz ignores a new \`value\`, \`rotation\` or \`zoom\`, so a slow render cannot fight the finger. To snap the crop after a gesture, write it in \`onValueCommit\`. A new \`aspect\` applies at once. The \`aspect\`, \`rotation\` and \`zoom\` from the first render become the reset target.

A prop acts when it changes. \`zoom\` also acts when a photo of another size arrives: with \`zoom={3}\` held, the crop goes back to 3 and commits with reason \`coverage\`. A write ignored during a gesture is not replayed. To limit a gesture, call \`details.cancel()\`. \`rotation={5}\` with no handler sets the angle once and the user can move away from it.

A photo after \`null\` counts as a first photo: zriz fits your \`value\` to it and sends nothing. Read \`getValue()\` if you keep the fitted crop.

## Turn it into text and back

Save a crop as text and read it back:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper, parse, serialize } from "@zriz/core";

const cropper = createCropper();
const text = serialize(cropper.getValue());
const saved = parse(text);
\`\`\`

\`serialize\` adds a version field, \`v: 1\`. \`parse\` takes the text or an object you already parsed, and returns \`null\` when it is not JSON, not an object or carries a \`v\` other than 1. A value with no \`v\` still reads. It fills missing fields with defaults, clamps \`rotation\` to 45 and \`perspective\` to 30 each way, and turns a bad \`aspect\` into \`"free"\`. The full flow is on [saving state](https://zriz.dev/docs/saving-state.md).

## Helpers

A constant and pure functions from \`@zriz/core\`:

| Export | What it does |
| - | - |
| \`defaultValue\` | Is the whole photo, level, with a free aspect |
| \`valuesEqual\` | Takes \`(a, b)\` and returns \`true\` when every field matches |
| \`changedFields\` | Takes \`(a, b)\` and returns the names of the fields that differ |
| \`aspectRatio\` | Takes \`(aspect, image, quarterTurns)\` and returns the ratio as a number, or \`null\` for a free aspect |
| \`valueToState\` | Takes \`(value, image, focal?)\` and returns the crop in image pixels. \`focal\` is a length in pixels and defaults to a 26 mm lens on the photo's long side |
| \`stateToValue\` | Takes \`(state, aspect)\` and turns a crop in image pixels back into a value |
"
`;

exports[`content > writes vanilla-js as markdown 1`] = `
"# Vanilla JS

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

Run the same cropper in plain JavaScript or any framework, with \`createCropper\` and the DOM binders.

## Install the core package

Add \`@zriz/core\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md). It has no dependencies, not even React.

## Write the markup

Give each element its \`data-zriz-item\`, so the stylesheet finds it, and give the viewport a width and a height. The binders set no role or name on the crop area, so write them in the markup:

\`\`\`html
<div id="cropper" data-zriz-item="root">
  <div
    id="viewport"
    data-zriz-item="viewport"
    style="max-width: 480px; height: 360px; padding: 24px"
  >
    <div id="photo" data-zriz-item="image"></div>
    <div id="mask" data-zriz-item="mask"></div>
    <div
      id="area"
      data-zriz-item="area"
      role="application"
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Crop area"
      style="position: absolute"
    ></div>
  </div>
  <div
    id="dial"
    data-zriz-item="dial"
    aria-label="Straighten"
    style="position: relative"
  >
    <div id="ticks" data-zriz-item="dial-ticks"></div>
  </div>
</div>
<input id="file" type="file" aria-label="Choose a photo" accept="image/*" />
<p id="status" role="alert"></p>
\`\`\`

## Bind the parts

Create a cropper, bind each element, add the four corner handles, and attach the photo when the user picks a file. Save this as \`cropper.ts\`. The snippets below import \`cropper\` and \`cleanups\` from it:

\`\`\`ts
import { createCropper } from "@zriz/core";
import {
  attachImage,
  bindArea,
  bindHandle,
  bindMask,
  bindPhoto,
  bindState,
  bindViewport,
  loadImage,
} from "@zriz/core/dom";
import "@zriz/core/styles.css";

const byId = (id: string) =>
  document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;

export const cropper = createCropper({
  defaultValue: { aspect: 4 / 3 },
});

export const cleanups = [
  bindState(cropper, byId("cropper")),
  bindViewport(cropper, byId("viewport")),
  bindPhoto(cropper, byId("photo")),
  bindMask(cropper, byId("mask")),
  bindArea(cropper, byId("area")),
];

const corners = [
  "top-left",
  "top-right",
  "bottom-left",
  "bottom-right",
] as const;

for (const corner of corners) {
  const handle = document.createElement("div");

  handle.dataset.zrizItem = "handle";
  handle.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
  byId("area").append(handle);
  cleanups.push(bindHandle(cropper, handle, corner));
}

const input = byId("file") as HTMLInputElement;
let last: AbortController | undefined;

const onFile = async () => {
  const file = input.files?.[0];

  if (!file) return;
  last?.abort();
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const { signal } = controller;

  last = controller;
  byId("status").textContent = "";

  try {
    attachImage(cropper, await loadImage(file, { signal }));
  } catch {
    if (signal.aborted) return;
    byId("status").textContent = "this photo does not open.";
  }
};

input.addEventListener("change", onFile);

cleanups.push(() => {
  last?.abort();
  input.removeEventListener("change", onFile);
});
\`\`\`

Each binder writes geometry inline and returns its cleanup. \`bindHandle\` also takes \`"top"\`, \`"right"\`, \`"bottom"\` and \`"left"\` for the edge bars. After you pick a file, the photo shows under a 4:3 crop. The handles have no layout until the next section places them.

Each pick aborts the load before it, so a slow file never replaces a newer one, and clears the old message. A cleanup aborts the last load and removes the listener. When a file does not open, \`loadImage\` rejects with a \`ZrizError\`, and [errors](https://zriz.dev/docs/errors.md) lists its codes.

## Place the handles

\`bindHandle\` wires the drag but leaves layout to you. This CSS centres a 44 px hit box on each corner and on the middle of each edge, and \`--zriz-handle-size\` on the area resizes it as on [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md):

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="handle"] {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: var(--zriz-handle-size, 44px);
  height: var(--zriz-handle-size, 44px);
  translate: -50% -50%;
}
[data-edge$="right"] {
  left: 100%;
}
[data-edge^="bottom"] {
  top: 100%;
}
[data-edge="top"],
[data-edge="bottom"] {
  left: 50%;
}
[data-edge="left"],
[data-edge="right"] {
  top: 50%;
}
\`\`\`

## Move the crop with keys

\`bindArea\` moves the crop but listens to no keys. Pass the area's events to \`areaKeyHandlers\`:

\`\`\`ts
import { areaKeyHandlers } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { cropper } from "./cropper";

const area = document.getElementById("area") as HTMLElement;
const keys = areaKeyHandlers(cropper);

area.onkeydown = keys.onKeyDown;
area.onkeyup = keys.onKeyUp;
area.onblur = keys.onBlur;

export const unbind = () => {
  keys.release();
};
\`\`\`

The keys are the ones on [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md). Call \`keys.release()\` when you unbind, so a key held at that moment does not lock the cropper. \`zoomHandlers\` and \`handleHandlers\` have the same \`release\`.

## Add the dial

\`bindDial\` handles drags, keys and the slider's ARIA values. \`bindDialTicks\` fills the strip with 41 ticks and moves it:

\`\`\`ts
import { bindDial, bindDialTicks } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { cleanups, cropper } from "./cropper";

const dial = document.getElementById("dial") as HTMLElement;
const ticks = document.getElementById("ticks") as HTMLElement;

cleanups.push(
  bindDial(cropper, dial),
  bindDialTicks(cropper, ticks)
);
\`\`\`

On a right-to-left page, the arrows and the drag swap. Pass \`{ dir: "rtl" }\` or \`{ dir: "ltr" }\` as the third argument of \`bindDial\` to set it yourself.

The same argument takes \`locale\` and \`format\` for the spoken angle, and \`ownText: true\` to keep an \`aria-valuetext\` of yours, which \`bindDial\` overwrites otherwise. It also takes \`gain\`, the pixels of drag per degree, 4 by default, \`slop\`, the pixels a drag travels before the dial moves, 10 by default, and \`tau\`, the time constant of a flick in ms, 95 by default.

After a flick, the speed falls to about a third every \`tau\` ms, and the glide stops once it drops under 0.5° a second. It ends about the flick's speed times \`tau\` past where you let go, so a flick at 100° a second glides about 9.5°.

\`locale\`, \`format\` and \`ownText\` belong to that element. \`dir\`, \`gain\`, \`slop\` and \`tau\` belong to the cropper: a second \`bindDial\` merges its values over the first, and both dials follow them. \`bindDialTicks\` spaces the ticks \`gain\` times 2.25 px apart, so it redraws when \`gain\` changes. When the last dial unbinds, the defaults come back.

\`bindDialTicks(cropper, strip, count)\` makes 41 ticks by default, and uses the children when the strip already has some.

## Add a zoom slider

Zoom has no single binder. Give the element \`role="slider"\`, \`tabindex="0"\`, an \`aria-label\`, a size, \`position: relative\`, \`touch-action: none\` and \`user-select: none\`, and draw the thumb yourself. The stylesheet paints nothing on zoom:

\`\`\`html
<div
  id="zoom"
  data-zriz-item="zoom"
  role="slider"
  tabindex="0"
  aria-label="Zoom"
  style="
    position: relative;
    width: 200px;
    height: 24px;
    touch-action: none;
    user-select: none;
    -webkit-user-select: none;
  "
>
  <div
    id="thumb"
    data-zriz-item="zoom-thumb"
    style="
      top: 4px;
      width: 16px;
      height: 16px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      background: currentColor;
      translate: -50%;
    "
  ></div>
</div>
\`\`\`

Forced colours mode drops the colour on the thumb. Add this rule to your stylesheet to paint it in \`CanvasText\`. Keep it in a CSS file, because a strict \`style-src\` refuses a \`<style>\` element. The thumb's colour is inline, so the rule needs \`!important\`:

\`\`\`css
@media (forced-colors: active) {
  [data-zriz-item="zoom-thumb"] {
    background: CanvasText !important;
  }
}
\`\`\`

Then hand its events to \`zoomHandlers\`:

\`\`\`ts
import {
  bindZoomThumb,
  zoomAria,
  zoomHandlers,
} from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { cleanups, cropper } from "./cropper";

const zoom = document.getElementById("zoom") as HTMLElement;
const thumb = document.getElementById("thumb") as HTMLElement;
const handlers = zoomHandlers(cropper);

zoom.onpointerdown = handlers.onPointerDown;
zoom.onpointermove = handlers.onPointerMove;
zoom.onpointerup = handlers.onPointerUp;
zoom.onpointercancel = handlers.onPointerCancel;
zoom.onlostpointercapture = handlers.onLostPointerCapture;
zoom.onkeydown = handlers.onKeyDown;
zoom.onkeyup = handlers.onKeyUp;
zoom.onblur = handlers.onBlur;

cleanups.push(
  zoomAria(cropper, zoom),
  bindZoomThumb(cropper, thumb)
);
\`\`\`

\`zoomAria\` keeps the slider's values current, and \`bindZoomThumb\` moves the thumb from 0% at 1× to 100% at the largest zoom. To end the track early, pass the same \`max\` to all three: \`zoomHandlers(cropper, { max, dir })\`, \`zoomAria(cropper, zoom, { max, locale, format, ownText })\` and \`bindZoomThumb(cropper, thumb, { max })\`. Without \`ownText: true\`, \`zoomAria\` overwrites your \`aria-valuetext\`.

## Read the crop

The \`commit\` event fires once per gesture with the crop value and the export plan, which is \`null\` until a photo loads:

\`\`\`ts
import { cropper } from "./cropper";

cropper.on("commit", (value, details) => {
  console.log(value.size, details.pixelCrop);
});
\`\`\`

To turn the crop into a file, pass the cropper to \`exportCrop\` as in [exporting](https://zriz.dev/docs/exporting.md). The first photo sends no \`commit\` until someone touches it, and a later photo sends one only when it has to refit the crop, see [events](https://zriz.dev/docs/events.md).

## Connect the rest

| Part | How to wire it |
| - | - |
| Dial value | \`bindDialValue(cropper, element, format)\` writes the angle as text. Without \`format\`, it writes whole degrees |
| Grid | No binder. \`bindState\` writes \`data-grid\` on the root as \`"none"\`, \`"coarse"\` or \`"fine"\`. Draw the lines in CSS. The stylesheet keeps \`data-zriz-item="grid"\` at opacity 0 without \`data-visible\`, and no binder sets it, so set \`opacity\` from \`[data-grid]\` on the root or leave that attribute off the element |
| State attributes | \`bindState\` writes every attribute [root](https://zriz.dev/docs/root.md) lists on your element, except \`data-shape\`. Set that one in your markup. Only the React parts write \`data-interacting\` on the area, \`data-active\` on a handle, \`data-ready\` on the image, \`data-dragging\` on zoom and \`data-shape\` on the area and mask. Leave out the fade rule on [viewport](https://zriz.dev/docs/viewport.md), which would keep the photo hidden here |
| Buttons | No binder. Call \`rotate90()\`, \`flip()\`, \`reset()\` or \`setAspect(1)\` on the cropper from a click |
| Your own img | \`bindImage(cropper, element)\` sizes and moves an element of yours with the photo. You set its \`src\` |
| Round crop | Pass \`"circle"\` as the third argument of \`bindMask\` and \`{ shape: "circle" }\` as the third argument of \`bindArea\`. Set \`aspect: 1\` in \`defaultValue\`. The stylesheet hides the corner handles when the area has \`data-shape="circle"\`, which \`bindArea\` leaves to your markup |
| Wheel and twist | Pass \`{ wheel: "zoom", rotateGesture: false }\` as the third argument of \`bindViewport\`. \`wheel\` also takes \`"pan"\` and \`"none"\` |
| Motion | Call \`viewOf(cropper).setOptions({ motion: "reduce", recenterDelay: 800 })\`. \`motion\` also takes \`"auto"\` and \`"always"\`. Each call replaces both options |
| Live variables | Pass \`{ liveVariables: true }\` as the third argument of \`bindArea\`, see [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md) |
| Announcements | No binder. On \`commit\`, write your own text into an element with \`role="status"\`, and point the area's \`aria-describedby\` at your instructions |

## Tear it down

Call every cleanup, detach the photo so its memory is freed, then destroy the cropper:

\`\`\`ts
import { attachImage } from "@zriz/core/dom";
import { cleanups, cropper } from "./cropper";

for (const cleanup of cleanups) cleanup();
attachImage(cropper, null);
cropper.destroy();
\`\`\`

\`destroy()\` also runs each plugin's \`destroy\`.
"
`;

exports[`content > writes viewport as markdown 1`] = `
"# Viewport

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

\`Viewport\` takes the gestures, \`Image\` draws the photo in it and \`Mask\` dims everything outside the crop.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/viewport runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, a track that sets the wheel to pan or zoom, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Anatomy

\`Viewport\` goes inside \`Root\`, with \`Image\`, \`Mask\` and \`Area\` inside it. Its width fills the parent like any block, so give it a height:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Viewport style={{ height: 400 }}>
  <Cropper.Image />
  <Cropper.Mask />
  <Cropper.Area aria-label="Crop area" />
</Cropper.Viewport>
\`\`\`

One \`Viewport\` per cropper. A second one takes over the layout, and zriz warns in development. Draw a live preview with \`useCropperFrame\`.

zriz makes it \`position: relative\` when it is static and sets \`touch-action: none\`, so the page does not scroll under a finger. \`Image\` clips the photo to the box by itself. The stylesheet also clips the viewport, so a handle at its edge never hangs outside it.

The track in the demo above sets \`wheel\` to pan or zoom. The photo pans once you zoom in. The demo uses the styles from the zriz site.

## Leave room around the crop

zriz fits the crop inside the viewport's padding. Without padding the crop touches the edges, and the clip cuts the handles in half. 24 px leaves room for the 44 px hit box to grab, see [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md):

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="viewport"] {
  padding: 24px;
}
\`\`\`

The crop fits again whenever the viewport changes size.

## Put it in a dialog

Centre the dialog with \`inset: 0\` and \`margin: auto\`. A percent translate on an odd size puts everything inside on a half pixel, and at 1x Chromium blurs the crop's outline.

## Choose what the wheel does

A plain wheel or two-finger scroll pans the photo. \`wheel="zoom"\` makes it zoom, and \`wheel="none"\` leaves it to the page. Pinch and Ctrl + wheel zoom in every mode:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Viewport wheel="none" style={{ height: 400 }} />
\`\`\`

## Turn off the two-finger twist

A twist straightens the photo once it passes 5°. To keep two fingers for pinch only:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Viewport
  rotateGesture={false}
  style={{ height: 400 }}
/>
\`\`\`

## Fade the photo in when it loads

\`Image\` gets \`data-ready\` once the cropper knows the photo's size: when it decodes, or at once when you pass \`image\` to \`Root\`. Hide it until then:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="image"] {
  transition: opacity 0.2s;
}
[data-zriz-item="image"]:not([data-ready]) {
  opacity: 0;
}
\`\`\`

## Darken the mask

The stylesheet paints the mask with \`--zriz-scrim\`. Set it to any colour:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="mask"] {
  --zriz-scrim: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7);
}
\`\`\`

## What each gesture does

\`Viewport\` handles pointer, touch and wheel input with no extra code:

| Input | What it does |
| - | - |
| Drag | Pans the photo under the crop |
| Pinch, trackpad pinch | Zooms, with a rubber band at both ends |
| Twist two fingers | Straightens once the twist passes 5° |
| Wheel, trackpad scroll | Pans by default. At the edge the page scrolls on. \`wheel\` changes it |
| Ctrl + wheel | Zooms, whatever \`wheel\` is set to |
| Escape during a drag | Cancels the drag and puts the photo back |

A press on a button, link, input or other control inside \`Viewport\` starts no pan, so its click still lands. The same goes for content you portal out of it.

A twist counts as a pinch, so \`Root\` gets \`data-pinching\` and the thirds grid shows. A trackpad twist works only in Safari. The crop's keys are on [crop area](https://zriz.dev/docs/crop-area.md).

## API

### \`Viewport\`

Renders a \`<div>\` inside \`Root\`. Gesture attributes like \`data-panning\` go on \`Root\`. Every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ interacting }\`.

- \`wheel\` \`"pan" | "zoom" | "none"\` = \`"pan"\`

  What a plain wheel or two-finger scroll does
- \`rotateGesture\` \`boolean\` = \`true\`

  Lets a two-finger twist straighten the photo

### \`Image\`

Renders a \`<div>\` inside \`Viewport\`. It holds an \`<img>\` that moves while you drag and a \`<canvas>\` drawn sharp at rest. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ ready }\`.

- \`alt\` \`string\` = \`""\`

  Names the photo for screen readers. Leave it empty when the crop is described elsewhere

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-ready\` | Once the cropper knows the photo's size: after it decodes, or at once with \`image\` on \`Root\` |
| \`data-sharp\` | While the sharp canvas frame shows |

### \`Mask\`

Renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` inside \`Viewport\`. It has no props of its own. \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ shape }\`.

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-shape\` | \`"rect"\` or \`"circle"\`, from \`Root\` |
| \`data-interacting\` | During a gesture and for a short hold after it, see [accessibility](https://zriz.dev/docs/accessibility.md) |

| Variable | What it sets |
| - | - |
| \`--zriz-scrim\` | Paints the mask outside the crop. [Styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md) lists its light and dark values |

The stylesheet fades it during a gesture, see [styling](https://zriz.dev/docs/styling.md).
"
`;

exports[`content > writes zoom as markdown 1`] = `
"# Zoom

> [llms.txt](https://zriz.dev/llms.txt)

A zoom slider and step buttons, for people with no wheel or pinch.

The page at https\\://zriz.dev/docs/zoom runs this cropper: a 3:2 photo, a zoom slider up to 3× with zoom out and zoom in, and open, reset and download in the corners.

## Anatomy

Put \`ZoomThumb\` inside \`Zoom\`. The steps go anywhere inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.ZoomStep factor={0.8} aria-label="Zoom out">
  −
</Cropper.ZoomStep>
<Cropper.Zoom
  aria-label="Zoom"
  style={{ width: 240, height: 24 }}
>
  <Cropper.ZoomThumb />
</Cropper.Zoom>
<Cropper.ZoomStep factor={1.25} aria-label="Zoom in">
  +
</Cropper.ZoomStep>
\`\`\`

zriz paints nothing here and gives these parts no size. Give \`Zoom\` a width and a height, then style the track and the thumb. A press on the track jumps the zoom to that point. [Full editor](https://zriz.dev/docs/full-editor.md) paints a track and a thumb you can copy.

The demo above stops the zoom at 3× with \`maxZoom\` on \`Root\`, marks a step at the limit as shown below and uses the styles from the zriz site.

## Centre the thumb

zriz sets the thumb's \`inset-inline-start\` on a log scale, so 2× sits 26% along a 15× track. This centres a round thumb on that point:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="zoom-thumb"] {
  top: 4px;
  width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  translate: -50% 0;
  background: #111;
}
@media (forced-colors: active) {
  [data-zriz-item="zoom-thumb"] {
    background: CanvasText;
  }
}
\`\`\`

In a right-to-left layout, use \`translate: 50% 0\`. Forced colours mode drops a plain background, so the second rule paints the thumb in \`CanvasText\`.

## Stop the slider at 5×

Pass the same \`max\` to \`Zoom\` and \`ZoomThumb\`. Pinch still reaches 15×:

\`\`\`tsx
<Cropper.Zoom
  max={5}
  aria-label="Zoom"
  style={{ width: 240, height: 24 }}
>
  <Cropper.ZoomThumb max={5} />
</Cropper.Zoom>
\`\`\`

To cap every way of zooming, set \`maxZoom\` on \`Root\` instead. Both parts follow it.

## Mark a step at the limit

At 1× and at \`maxZoom\`, a press does nothing, but the button stays enabled. A large \`minSize\` can stop the zoom below \`maxZoom\`, and \`useZoom\` does not report that stop, so this recipe does not mark it. \`useZoom\` tells you where the zoom is. Use \`aria-disabled\` for this, because \`disabled\` drops keyboard focus from the button at the limit. Render this inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useZoom } from "@zriz/react";
import * as Cropper from "@zriz/react/cropper";

export const ZoomSteps = () => {
  const zoom = useZoom();

  return (
    <>
      <Cropper.ZoomStep
        factor={0.8}
        aria-label="Zoom out"
        aria-disabled={zoom.value <= zoom.min || undefined}
      >
        −
      </Cropper.ZoomStep>
      <Cropper.ZoomStep
        factor={1.25}
        aria-label="Zoom in"
        aria-disabled={zoom.value >= zoom.max || undefined}
      >
        +
      </Cropper.ZoomStep>
    </>
  );
};
\`\`\`

The browser dims only \`disabled\`, so paint the off state yourself:

\`\`\`css
[data-zriz-item="zoom-step"][aria-disabled="true"] {
  opacity: 0.4;
}
\`\`\`

## Build your own slider

\`useZoom\` hands a slider of your own the zoom and a commit callback. This one is a range input. Render it inside \`Root\`:

\`\`\`tsx
"use client";

import { useZoom } from "@zriz/react";

export const ZoomSlider = () => {
  const zoom = useZoom();

  return (
    <input
      type="range"
      aria-label="Zoom"
      min={zoom.min}
      max={zoom.max}
      step={0.01}
      value={zoom.value}
      onChange={(e) =>
        zoom.onValueChange(e.target.valueAsNumber)
      }
      onPointerUp={zoom.onValueCommitted}
      onPointerCancel={zoom.onValueCommitted}
      onKeyUp={zoom.onValueCommitted}
    />
  );
};
\`\`\`

A range input moves in even steps, so 2× sits 7% along a 15× track. \`Zoom\` uses a log scale.

[Hooks](https://zriz.dev/docs/hooks.md) lists every field \`useZoom\` returns.

## Keyboard

These keys work while \`Zoom\` has focus:

| Key | What it does |
| - | - |
| \`left\` or \`right\` | Move 1/20 of the track. Right zooms in, left in RTL |
| \`up\` or \`down\` | Move 1/20 of the track. Up zooms in |
| \`shift\` + \`arrow keys\` | Move half the track |
| \`page up\` or \`page down\` | The same as Shift and an arrow key |
| \`home\` or \`end\` | Go to 1× or to \`max\` |

Each step multiplies the zoom by the same amount, about 1.14× on a 15× track.

Escape during a drag cancels it and puts the zoom back.

## API

### \`Zoom\`

Renders a \`<div role="slider">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. Every part also takes \`className\`, \`render\` and the props of its element, see [composition](https://zriz.dev/docs/composition.md). \`className\` and \`render\` get \`{ dragging }\`.

- \`aria-label\` \`string\`

  Names the slider for screen readers. Development builds warn without it
- \`max\` \`number\`

  The zoom at the end of the track. Defaults to \`maxZoom\` on \`Root\`
- \`format\` \`Intl.NumberFormatOptions\`

  How screen readers hear the zoom. By default 2 reads as \`"200%"\`. An \`aria-valuetext\` of yours wins over it

| Attribute | When |
| - | - |
| \`data-dragging\` | While a pointer holds any \`Zoom\` track of this cropper or a \`useZoom\` slider moves, so a second copy lights up too |

### \`ZoomThumb\`

Renders an \`aria-hidden\` \`<div>\` inside \`Zoom\`. zriz writes \`position: absolute\` and \`inset-inline-start\`, from 0% at 1× to 100% at \`max\`.

- \`max\` \`number\`

  The zoom at the end of the track. Defaults to \`maxZoom\` on \`Root\`. Keep it equal to \`max\` on \`Zoom\`

### \`ZoomStep\`

Renders a \`<button type="button">\` anywhere inside \`Root\`. Each press jumps at once, with no animation and no grid.

- \`factor\` \`number\` required

  Multiplies the zoom on each press. Below 1 zooms out, so 0.8 undoes 1.25
"
`;
